// Faith
children.push(H1("The Lupine Matron and Imperial Faith"));
children.push(P("The Lupine Matron is the divine patron of the Zhuvedian Empire, represented as a great wolf \u2014 wise, protective, and fierce. According to Zhuvedian theology, the Lupine Matron gave the empire\u2019s people their core virtues: loyalty, honor, wisdom, and the strength to endure hardship. The phrase \u201CWe are the Kin of Great Timberwolf\u201D references this divine connection and appears in imperial prayers and ceremonies."));
children.push(P("The Matron\u2019s worship long predates the empire that now venerates her. Zhuvedian doctrine holds that she has guided many champions and many peoples across a span of time far longer than any dynasty\u2019s recorded history \u2014 Zhuvedus was simply the most successful and most enduring of those she chose to favor. Whether Zhuvedus himself ever attained true divinity, however, is a matter of genuine theological dispute, one that runs directly through the empire\u2019s politics and its faith alike (see The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath)."));
children.push(P("The faith of the Lupine Matron has historically unified the empire, providing moral and spiritual justification for imperial authority. The Golden Tablets, containing the fundamental laws of the empire, are said to have been inspired by the Lupine Matron\u2019s wisdom and committed to writing by the empire\u2019s forefathers."));
children.push(P("However, as the empire fractured politically, regional variations in the faith have emerged. Some breakaway territories emphasize different aspects of the Lupine Matron\u2019s teachings or venerate local saints above imperial doctrine. This theological fragmentation mirrors the political splintering and presents both a challenge and an opportunity for Qilvayas\u2019s restoration efforts."));
children.push(P("The Church of the Lupine Matron in the capital remains the institutional heart of the faith \u2014 wealthy, hierarchical, and deeply entangled with imperial politics. Its clergy administer doctrine, sanctify imperial authority, and guard their interpretive monopoly over omens, visions, and the divine will. Those who claim direct revelation outside Church sanction occupy dangerous ground: not quite heretical, but never fully tolerated. This tension becomes immediately relevant to the party through the Seeress (see Key NPCs and Factions)."));

children.push(H2("The Church in Practice"));
children.push(P("The capital Church is governed by the Matriarchate \u2014 Matriarch Ilsevet Corvane as the Voice of the Matron in Aenodira \u2014 advised (and constrained) by the Synod of the Grey, a council of senior clergy whose sessions are closed and whose politics are not. There is no supreme authority above them, and this is a decision rather than a vacancy: the Matriarchate is collegial by design, so that no one throat holds the whole of the faith. A Prelate accordingly holds his jurisdiction from the Synod rather than from the Matriarch personally \u2014 her colleague with his own writ, not her subordinate, which is why the Office of Omens can pursue a posture she has not endorsed and cannot simply countermand. Beneath them: the seminaries of the Sanctum, the parish structure that reaches into every district and most loyalist provinces, and the Office of Omens, headed by Prelate Sarvin Odell, which claims sole authority to receive, examine, and rule on visions, portents, and claims of divine contact. The Office\u2019s method is bureaucratic rather than cruel \u2014 polite reception, separate questioning, a written determination \u2014 and its determinations have a way of ending in files rather than answers. It resents Vaelindra\u2019s existence the way a licensing board resents an unlicensed master: not because she is fraudulent, but because she is not theirs. And under Odell, resentment has recently become policy: as unsanctioned claims multiply across the fracturing empire, the Office has moved from keeping files to serving writs \u2014 its posture toward Vaelindra, and toward anyone publicly associated with her, is now active interference rather than watchful patience (engaged in Sessions Five and Six)."));
children.push(H2("The Keepers of the Ascent (Imperial Cult)"));
children.push(P("The imperial cult\u2019s formal body is small: the Keepers of the Ascent, a priesthood of perhaps forty, led by Hierophant Malzeth Corr, maintaining the dynastic shrine within Highcourt and the doctrine that Zhuvedus rose to divinity and his line carries the spark of it. The Keepers serve the throne\u2019s legitimacy and know it; the Church tolerates them and both institutions understand exactly why. Qilvayas\u2019s own relationship to the cult is correct rather than warm \u2014 he performs the dynastic rites because an emperor must, and the question of whether he privately believes them is one his court has learned not to ask."));

children.push(H2("Death, the Vigil, and the Halls of the Dead"));
children.push(P("Doctrine is specific about one thing across every schism, every breakaway see, every province that argues about everything else: the Matron watches the dead home, and she does it at night. No Zhuvedian burns, buries, or gives a body to the water before it has been kept through at least one full night\u2019s Vigil \u2014 watched, lit, and never left alone \u2014 because a soul let into the dark unwatched is a soul the Matron might lose track of, and doctrine is not interested in finding out whether that\u2019s literally true. This is the one funerary practice that travels unchanged from the capital\u2019s golden dome to the furthest Ardven monastery-burgh: something recognizably vigil-shaped happens everywhere, even where almost nothing else about the rite matches."));
children.push(P("What happens after the Vigil is where the empire stops agreeing with itself, the same way it stops agreeing about everything else once you leave the Crownlands. Aenodira interns its dead in the Bone Galleries beneath the Old Forum, centuries deep, oldest at the bottom \u2014 a practice old enough to predate the Church\u2019s involvement in it. (The public stairs on the Forum\u2019s east side serve the upper galleries only; the deeper levels have been closed \u2018for consolidation\u2019 since before living memory, and the vigil-keepers do not ask \u2014 which keeps the funerary traffic entirely clear of the route established in Session Six.) The Suthmark folds burial into the planting calendar: the devout are put into the ground on saints\u2019 days tied to sowing or harvest, on the theory that a soul returned to the earth on the right day helps the next year\u2019s grain along. Tarnovar barely buries at all in the imperial sense \u2014 a dead Tarnovari\u2019s oaths, not their body, are what the living formally inherit, spoken aloud at the standing stones by whoever takes up the unfinished promise, while the body itself goes to the Old Wood by older and less documented means. Velmareth, characteristically, sends its dead into the reed-sea on lit barges and calls the crocodiles a hazard rather than a horror."));
children.push(P("For an ordinary market town with no catacombs and no delta to send anyone into, the physical answer is the Vigil Hall \u2014 a modest, unglamorous building near the local shrine or almonry, kept by lay vigil-keepers (traditionally widowed or elderly, traditionally paid almost nothing, traditionally among the best-informed people in town about who just died owing what to whom). Every major town has one. It is exactly the kind of small, load-bearing institution that can hold a scene, a rumor, or a body the party very badly needs to examine, without a word of this needing to touch the Undercourt, the binding site, or anything else still sealed for later."));

// ==================== PART I: MAGIC & THE FAITH ====================
// ============ THE ASPECTS AND THE SAINTS ============
children.push(H1("The Aspects and the Canon of Saints"));
children.push(P("The Matron is one. This is the first thing a Zhuvedian child learns and the last thing a Zhuvedian heretic denies, and the empire has argued about almost everything else instead. What the faith does have \u2014 what any faith serving a whole civilization eventually acquires \u2014 is a vocabulary for the different things one goddess does, and a long roll of people she is held to have done them through."));

children.push(H2("The Doctrine of Aspects"));
children.push(P("An Aspect is an office the Matron holds, not a part she is divided into. The distinction sounds like hairsplitting and is in fact the sharpest doctrinal line in the empire, because two centuries of schism have taught the Church exactly how a metaphor becomes a second god. Orthodoxy is carried by a preposition: one says the Matron in her Hunt, never the Matron of the Hunt as though there were another Matron elsewhere doing something else. The heresy of Division \u2014 teaching the Aspects as distinct powers \u2014 was condemned four hundred years ago and is still the charge the Sanctum reaches for first, which tells you how often somebody drifts into it by accident on a cold night in a country parish."));
children.push(P("For a cleric, the Aspect is the shape of the calling rather than its source. The power is the Matron\u2019s; the Aspect is which of her offices the caller was answered from, and it determines the color of the vestment, the wording of the daily office, and which shrine will feed you when you arrive wet and unannounced. Sanction is issued against an Aspect: a warrant-medal is stamped on its reverse with the Aspect\u2019s mark, and a Watch cleric ministering as a Hunt cleric is committing a paperwork offence, not a sin."));

children.push(H2("The Seven Recognized Aspects"));
children.push(P("Seven Aspects hold formal recognition from the Matriarchate. The correspondence to a cleric\u2019s domain is exact and unremarkable to anyone in the world; the table below is for the table, not for the fiction."));
children.push(table(
  ["Aspect", "Domain", "Who Serves It, and How She Is Shown"],
  [28, 23, 49],
  [
    ["The Watch at the Threshold", "Life", "Vigil-keepers, hospitallers, almonry clergy, midwives \u2014 the commonest calling in the empire by a wide margin. Shown as a wolf lying across a doorway, a lamp behind it."],
    ["The Long Hunt", "War", "Legion chaplains, the Church militant, Censor-Captains, anyone whose duty is endurance rather than fury. Shown as a wolf at a steady trot, never at a sprint."],
    ["The Long Memory", "Knowledge", "Archivists, jurists, the Chartered Scriptorium, and \u2014 to the irritation of everyone else \u2014 the Office of Omens. Shown as a wolf with its head turned back over its shoulder."],
    ["The Lamp Left Burning", "Light", "Vigil-hall lamplighters, lighthouse and channel-light keepers, the night offices. Shown as a lit lamp, and an eye reflecting it."],
    ["The Elder Range", "Nature", "The Old Observance, field-shrine keepers, druids and rangers who never asked anyone\u2019s permission. Shown with no icon at all: a bare stone, a marked tree."],
    ["The Winter Voice", "Tempest", "The northern liturgies, storm-country parishes, Orlath, and the Ardven monastery-burghs. Shown as an open throat, the howl drawn as visible breath."],
    ["The Limping Bitch", "Trickery", "Nobody, officially. Everybody, in the provinces. Shown as a she-wolf dragging a hind leg."]
  ]
));
children.push(B("The Limping Bitch.", "The seventh is the one worth a paragraph. She is the she-wolf who feigns a broken leg to draw the hunter away from the den \u2014 deception spent entirely in defence of what is behind her. She has never been formally recognized and has never once been suppressed. She is not painted in a single cathedral in the Crownlands and she is painted on roughly half the barn doors in the Suthmark. Ask a Prelate about her and you will get a careful sentence about folk piety; ask a village woman and she will tell you which of her neighbours the Matron limped for. A Trickery cleric in this empire is entirely real, entirely unlicensed, and entirely unsurprising to anyone outside the Sanctum."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the Limping Bitch is the closest the orthodox faith comes to admitting that a lie told to protect the den is not the same crime as an oath broken for advantage. That distinction is the campaign\u2019s whole moral engine, sitting in plain sight on a barn door, and no theologian in the empire has connected it to the Founding. If a player character ever draws the line out loud in front of clergy, the room should go very quiet." }]));

children.push(H2("The Aspects the Canon Does Not Have"));
children.push(P("Tables that use domains beyond the 2014 Player\u2019s Handbook will find the empire has opinions, and the gaps are as informative as the entries."));
children.push(table(
  ["Domain", "Standing in the Empire"],
  [20, 80],
  [
    ["Grave", "The Watch in its darker register \u2014 the Threshold-Wardens, who deal with what the Vigil exists to prevent when the Vigil has already failed. Recognized, small, and nobody\u2019s first choice of dinner guest."],
    ["Forge", "The Teeth of the Mountain: the Stonesworn\u2019s Aspect, recognized fully in Tarnovar and not at all in the Crownlands, since recognizing it would concede that Tarnovar\u2019s clergy are clergy."],
    ["Order", "The Packlaw itself, venerated as an office rather than a doctrine. Favored by magistrates, and by exactly the sort of person who becomes one."],
    ["Nature", "See The Elder Range \u2014 the Old Observance\u2019s Aspect. The Church\u2019s position remains studied non-comment, for reasons the DM understands better than the Synod does."],
    ["Death", "There is no Aspect, and the absence is deliberate doctrine rather than an oversight. Divine power answering to death in this empire is not answering from the Matron, and the Office of Omens keeps a file classification open against the day someone proves it."],
    ["Arcana", "No Aspect, and a jurisdictional war waiting to happen. Thaumaturgy is chartered, not blessed; a cleric of Arcana would set the Charter and the Sanction arguing for a decade over which of them had been insulted."]
  ]
));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "a player who brings a Death cleric to this table is not doing something the setting forbids. They are doing something the setting has been waiting for. The honest answer to who answers that call is open, and the thing beneath the Old Forum is at least as plausible a candidate as anything the Sanctum has filed. Do not decide it early, and do not tell them there is a question." }]));

children.push(H2("Oaths and the Paladin"));
children.push(P("In a world where a sworn word has literal weight, a paladin is not a knight with spells. A paladin is a person carrying a binding heavy enough to be felt, and the empire treats them accordingly: with respect, with wariness, and with paperwork."));
children.push(BUL("Devotion:", "the Crown-sworn and the Church\u2019s own Oathwards. The most institutionally comfortable of the oaths and the most closely watched, because a Devotion paladin who concludes his institution is the thing in breach becomes the empire\u2019s least manageable problem. Emperor Qilvayas is Crown-sworn."));
children.push(BUL("The Ancients:", "the Green Watch \u2014 the Old Observance\u2019s paladins, sworn at field-shrines and standing stones rather than altars, holding warrants nobody issued. Tolerated warmly in the provinces, received in the Sanctum the way a magistrate receives a folk remedy."));
children.push(BUL("Vengeance:", "the Wolf-Price made a vocation. Recognized in the provinces, where blood debt is a functioning legal category, and quietly deplored at court, where it is an embarrassment with a sword. Ban Dregan Morn is what this oath looks like when nobody supervises it for thirty years."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only \u2014 the Oathbreaker: "), { t: "the DMG\u2019s Oathbreaker is not a subclass in this setting so much as a diagnosis. A paladin who breaks his oath and keeps the power is walking, step for step, the road the Founder walked, and this world\u2019s metaphysics say that road leads somewhere specific and occupied. If a player goes there, the shadow beneath Aenodira notices immediately, and what it offers will be generous. This is a campaign-altering event and should be played as one, not as a mechanical swap." }]));

children.push(H2("The Canon of Saints"));
children.push(P("Canonization in the Zhuvedian Church requires three things: a documented life, a testified act, and the passage of a full generation, so that nobody is made a saint by the people who liked them. The Synod of the Grey rules; the Matriarch confirms; the parishes, in practice, decide first and let the paperwork catch up. Fourteen names carry the whole empire\u2019s devotion, and the roll below is what a literate Zhuvedian would be able to recite badly and a village priest perfectly."));
children.push(table(
  ["Feast", "Saint, and What They Are For"],
  [26, 74],
  [
    ["Wolfmoon 1", "Lupenna of the First Shrine \u2014 hospitality, guest-right, roadside shrines"],
    ["Wolfmoon 4", "Ivessa of the Ford \u2014 rearguards, ferrymen, and anyone who says go on without me"],
    ["Thawtide 11", "Coren the Unhurried \u2014 vigil-keepers, night-workers, and the patient"],
    ["Sowmonth 8", "Yenna Corvane \u2014 advocates and the badly represented"],
    ["Sowmonth 27", "Perisse the Milkless \u2014 foundlings, nurses, and the bound-freed"],
    ["Haymonth 9", "Vosk the Field-Cutter \u2014 surgeons, medics, and their consciences"],
    ["Harvestide 2", "Bellara of Ambervale \u2014 harvests, almoners, and honest thieves"],
    ["Vinmoon 3", "Ilvane the Sea-Kept \u2014 pilots, harbor-hands, and merchants who honor a bad contract"],
    ["Fallowmonth 21", "Halvard of the Quiet Rite \u2014 stonewrights, record-keepers, unglamorous fidelity"],
    ["Greywane 14", "Tobrin Ashfall \u2014 the falsely accused"],
    ["Greywane 30", "Elleth of the Grey Gate \u2014 gatekeepers, quarantine wardens, the unforgivable choice"],
    ["Longdark 19", "Odarr of the Nine Winters \u2014 winter travelers, the maimed, the stubborn"],
    ["Threshold 6", "Marek the Witness \u2014 witnesses, oath-keepers, inconvenient truth"],
    ["struck", "Ovric, Struck from the Canon \u2014 nothing, officially"]
  ]
));
children.push(H3("The Lives"));
children.push(B("Lupenna of the First Shrine.", "The woman at whose roadside fire the Matron was first fed by a Zhuvedian hand. Her feast opens the year. Shown as a bowl set outside a door."));
children.push(B("Ivessa of the Ford.", "Held a river crossing through one night so a village could get behind her. Found at dawn still upright, dead, spear planted in the gravel. Three separate towns claim the ford, and the Church has never adjudicated, because settling it would require ruling on which river she died in."));
children.push(B("Coren the Unhurried.", "Kept four thousand nights\u2019 Vigil and refused ordination six times. Shown as a short wick over a very large reservoir of oil. Canonized over the objection of the Sanctum, which found his refusals insubordinate; the parishes did it anyway and the Matriarchate ratified a fait accompli."));
children.push(B("Yenna Corvane.", "A jurist who argued four hundred wolf-price cases for the poor and lost most of them deliberately, on the record, to build the precedent that would win the four hundred and first. Archjurist Vhal keeps a copy of her losses; it is the most annotated book in the Law Commission."));
children.push(B("Perisse the Milkless.", "Kept nine orphans alive through a siege, three of them not her people. Shown as nine cups, one of them cracked. Her cult is strongest among the fourth-tier bound, which is why the Church\u2019s enthusiasm for it is carefully measured."));
children.push(B("Vosk the Field-Cutter.", "A legion surgeon who refused to sort the wounded by rank and was broken for it twice. Shown as a bone-saw and a coin, the coin being refused. The tiered-care system Book Three establishes is precisely what he refused to do, and the legions\u2019 surgeons keep his icon anyway, which is either irony or protest."));
children.push(B("Bellara of Ambervale.", "Fed a famine district by miracle, or by emptying a duke\u2019s granary; the accounts differ and the Suthmark does not care which. Shown as an overturned grain measure."));
children.push(B("Ilvane the Sea-Kept.", "Drowned getting a rival\u2019s crew off a wreck she had every commercial reason to leave. Shown as a rope in an open hand, untied. The Compact keeps her feast as a trading holiday and almost nobody goes to the shrine."));
children.push(B("Halvard of the Quiet Rite.", "A Stonesworn dwarf who recut an entire oath-stone field alone over eleven years after a landslide, so that no family\u2019s oath would go unmarked. Shown as a chisel worn to a stub. Tarnovar keeps him; the Crownlands has never canonized him, because canonizing a Tarnovari saint would concede something."));
children.push(B("Tobrin Ashfall.", "A hedge-priest who talked a town out of burning a girl for lycanthropy, then spent forty years proving he had been right. Shown as an unlit pyre, and still the least-preached saint in the Crownlands."));
children.push(B("Elleth of the Grey Gate.", "Kept Aenodira\u2019s plague gate and decided who came in. Shown as a gate half shut. What she is actually venerated for is the ones she turned away."));
children.push(B("Odarr of the Nine Winters.", "Kept the northern passes open for pilgrims through nine hard winters, lost both feet to the sixth, and did the last three on a sledge. Shown as a sledge and two empty boots. Orlath claims him hardest."));
children.push(B("Marek the Witness.", "Saw a lord break a parley-oath, was invited to say he had seen nothing, and said what he saw. Executed for it. Shown as an open eye above a closed mouth."));
children.push(B("Ovric, Struck from the Canon.", "Venerated for two centuries; removed by decree of the Synod ninety years ago, for reasons the decree does not state. The Ostmark parishes never stopped. Shown as the blank where an icon was."));

children.push(H3("Three Saints Worth More Than a Row"));
children.push(B("Marek the Witness", "is the saint of this campaign\u2019s central mechanism, and nobody in the empire has noticed. His feast falls in Threshold, the liminal month when old oaths are renewed and old debts settled, which is either an accident of the calendar or the only piece of liturgical honesty the Church has ever committed by mistake. His icon shows the mouth closed because he said the thing once and never repeated it. Parties who ask a priest why a witness-saint is depicted silent will get the standard answer \u2014 that testimony given once truly needs no second telling \u2014 and it is the wrong answer, and the right one is not written down anywhere."));
children.push(B("Lupenna", "is the saint of hospitality, and the empire\u2019s two worst crimes in living memory are both violations of guest-right: the Vintage Night, where congregations were killed under a festival\u2019s welcome, and the murder that made Olvesa a Saint-Regent, where a lord was killed under his host\u2019s roof. The Church has never preached the connection. The See of Orlath preaches almost nothing else."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only \u2014 Ovric: "), { t: "he was struck because a Sanctum archivist working the deep stacks found what Ovric had actually witnessed and written down, and the Synod of the day chose the smaller scandal. The suppression decree is a masterpiece of saying nothing at four hundred words. The original file exists; it is in the Imperial Archive\u2019s closed levels, and it is the sort of thing a company holding the Lector\u2019s Seal could conceivably reach. What he witnessed touches the Founding. Do not spend this early, and do not make it the only route to the truth \u2014 it is a corroborating door, not the first one." }]));

children.push(H3("Relics, Feasts, and the Pilgrim Roads"));
children.push(P("Every saint on the roll has relics, and the relic economy already described in this book runs on them: certified fragments with provenance documents, uncertified fragments with better stories, and the Second Treasury at Orlath holding a rival set the Sanctum considers fraudulent and cannot inspect. A saint\u2019s feast is a working holiday in the parishes that keep it \u2014 markets, processions, a suspension of certain kinds of legal proceeding \u2014 and a scheduling problem for everyone else. Four pilgrim roads carry real traffic:"));
children.push(BUL("The Matron\u2019s Road:", "Aenodira to Lupenna, two days on foot, walked by tens of thousands a year and lined the whole way with candle-stalls, relic-hawkers, and the most competitive begging in the empire."));
children.push(BUL("The Penitent\u2019s Stair:", "the switchback ascent to the Reconciliation Shrine above Orlath. Crutches are left at the top; the town at the bottom sells them."));
children.push(BUL("The Nine Winters Way:", "Odarr\u2019s passes, walked in winter on purpose. The Ardven monastery-burghs maintain the stations, which is diplomacy dressed as piety and works as both."));
children.push(BUL("The Three Fords:", "three towns claim Ivessa\u2019s crossing and the Church has never adjudicated, because settling it would require ruling on which river she died in. Serious pilgrims walk all three and let the Matron sort it out."));

// ============ STANDING ============
children.push(H1("Standing: What the Empire Remembers"));
children.push(P("An institution is a memory with a building around it. In a world where sworn words carry literal weight, a party\u2019s relationship with a faction is not a reputation score in any abstract sense \u2014 it is the accumulated residue of small promises made and kept in that institution\u2019s sight. The Church calls it good odor. The legions call it a name. The Concord calls it a line of credit and means all three of the others."));
children.push(P("Standing is deliberately the lightest possible system: one number per faction, from zero to five, moved by the DM at the end of a session. There are no new rules to learn and nothing to look up mid-scene."));
children.push(table(
  ["Tier", "Name", "What It Means at the Table"],
  [11, 22, 67],
  [
    ["0", "Unknown", "You are a face. Doors open at the ordinary price and no faster."],
    ["1", "Noted", "Someone in the building knows your name. Minor favors are possible; you will be asked for one back."],
    ["2", "Trusted", "Advantage on Charisma checks made against members of this faction who know of you. Small requests are granted without a decision being taken upstairs."],
    ["3", "Relied Upon", "The faction brings you work rather than waiting to be asked. You gain access to one thing the faction controls and outsiders do not \u2014 a room, a record, a person, a route."],
    ["4", "Of the Body", "You are treated as internal. Its enemies are now yours whether you chose them or not, and its rivals adjust their opinion of you downward by one tier without meeting you."],
    ["5", "Named", "The faction will spend something real on you \u2014 money, people, or its own standing \u2014 once, and will expect the same. This is a promise, and in this world a promise is a weight."]
  ]
));

children.push(H2("The Factions"));
children.push(P("Twelve bodies are worth tracking in the campaign\u2019s first arc. Each moves on its own logic, and the last column is the thing a DM most needs at hand: what this faction will actually do for a party at Relied Upon."));
children.push(P("Each entry names what raises the number, what lowers it, and \u2014 the thing a DM most needs at hand \u2014 what the faction will actually do for a party at Relied Upon."));
children.push(B("The Lupine Throne and the Court.", "Rises when you make the restoration look competent, and you do it where the court can see. Falls when you embarrass it, or when you succeed so loudly that you become a faction of your own. At tier 3: an audience without the Lord Chamberlain arranging it first, which is a thing perhaps forty people in the empire have."));
children.push(B("The Church of the Lupine Matron.", "Rises when you honor the Vigil, protect clergy, or resolve a matter without the Church having to be seen to act. Falls when you minister unsanctioned, shelter a claimed visionary, or win an argument with a Prelate in public. At tier 3: parish hospitality anywhere in loyalist territory, and a sealed letter that ends most provincial difficulties."));
children.push(B("The Office of Omens.", "Rises when you bring them a claim, answer their questions, and are useful. Falls when you are useful to Vaelindra instead. At tier 3: nothing good \u2014 see below. This faction is the exception."));
children.push(B("The Imperial Academy.", "Rises when you bring back knowledge, students, or credit; publication counts. Falls when you bring back a scandal, or make the faculty look like the people who examined you. At tier 3: the deep stacks, a laboratory, and a professor\u2019s signature on a requisition nobody will question."));
children.push(B("The Law Commission.", "Rises when you produce evidence that survives contact with a hearing. Falls when you resolve things in ways that would not. At tier 3: Archjurist Vhal\u2019s ear, and a Writ she will sign on a day\u2019s notice."));
children.push(B("The Imperial Legions.", "Rises when you stand where you said you would stand. Falls when you leave; once is enough. At tier 3: a century\u2019s worth of soldiers who will listen, and a quartermaster who will lose the paperwork on what you take."));
children.push(B("The Capital Merchants\u2019 Concord.", "Rises when you make a route safer or a contract enforceable. Falls when you cost anyone money, including honestly. At tier 3: credit, a factor in every major town, and the second-best intelligence network in the empire."));
children.push(B("The Inkhands of Rivergate.", "Rises when you pay, shut up, and do not bring the Watch back with you. Falls on any of those three, reversed. At tier 3: unlicensed work at trade prices, and a door out of the district at any hour."));
children.push(B("Vaelindra\u2019s referral circle.", "Rises when you keep her confidence and keep your word. Falls when you spend her name for advantage. At tier 3: introduction to the others she has referred over fifty years, which is a stranger and more useful list than it sounds."));
children.push(B("The See of Orlath.", "Rises when you treat the schism as a disagreement between the faithful. Falls when you treat it as the Sanctum does. At tier 3: safe passage in the north, and the Second Treasury\u2019s doors."));
children.push(B("The Tarnovar envoy\u2019s circle.", "Rises when you keep an oath at cost, where a Tarnovari can see it. Falls when you break a small one, at no cost, where a Tarnovari can see it. At tier 3: a hearing at Kamenhold, which nobody from the empire has had in a century."));
children.push(B("The Golds and the Greys.", "Rises when you are seen at the Long Course, on a side, in the wrong weather. Falls when you are seen on the other side. At tier 3: three thousand people in a stand who will chant your name, which is a weapon and should be handled as one."));

children.push(H3("The Office of Omens Is Not Like the Others"));
children.push(P("Standing with the Office is the one score a party should be trying to keep low, and the table should never be told so directly. Every tier of it is real, functioning access \u2014 the Office is polite, efficient, and genuinely helpful to those it has a file on. What rises with the tier is not favor but attention, and attention from a body whose determinations end in files rather than answers is the campaign\u2019s quietest trap. At tier 3 the Office assigns a Censor to the party\u2019s affairs as a courtesy. At tier 5 it knows where they sleep, who referred them, and what they are afraid of, and it has written all three down."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "run Office standing openly and let the players see the number go up. Let it feel like progress. Prelate Odell will be warm, useful, and completely sincere, because he is: he believes the Office is the only thing standing between the empire and a hundred unlicensed prophets. The horror arrives the first time the party needs the Office not to know something and discovers how much it already does." }]));

children.push(H3("Standing Pulls Against Standing"));
children.push(P("The empire is not one body and cannot be pleased at once. When a party gains a tier with any of these, the DM should ask whether the opposed body noticed."));
children.push(table(
  ["Gaining Here", "Costs You With", "Because"],
  [25, 24, 51],
  [
    ["The See of Orlath", "The Church of the Lupine Matron", "There is exactly one licensing authority for the divine, and both of them are certain which."],
    ["Vaelindra\u2019s circle", "The Office of Omens", "The Office\u2019s objection to her is professional, not theological, which makes it worse."],
    ["The Inkhands", "The Law Commission", "Vhal\u2019s whole project is that the law should reach Rivergate. The Inkhands are the argument that it does not."],
    ["The Tarnovar envoy\u2019s circle", "The Lupine Throne and the Court", "Never again the wolf\u2019s word is a proverb about the people currently paying you."],
    ["The Golds", "The Greys", "And the reverse, and there is no third option, and the stands are how the capital says things it cannot say in the Forum."],
    ["The Concord", "The Legions", "Book Six\u2019s requisition powers are, from a merchant\u2019s chair, legalized theft with a drum behind it."]
  ]
));
children.push(P("Two pairings are deliberately not opposed, and the reason is worth a DM\u2019s attention. The Academy sits comfortably beside almost everything, which is what makes it the best cover in the empire and the reason a graduating cohort can move through every one of these rooms. And the Church and the Throne do not appear opposite each other anywhere on this table \u2014 not because they agree, but because neither can afford to make a party choose. The day one of them does is a campaign event."));
children.push(H1("Magic and the Word"));
children.push(H2("The Weight of the Word"));
children.push(P("The setting\u2019s metaphysical signature, formalized: in the world of the Lupine Matron, sworn words have literal weight. An oath spoken with intent creates a real, thin binding \u2014 imperceptible to almost everyone, cumulative across a civilization, and the reason this world\u2019s history keeps rhyming. It is why the Tarnovari swear on stone (stone holds what air forgets), why Ban Dregan\u2019s Fence works on the level of dread as well as deterrence, why Duke Norr\u2019s tens of thousands of personal oaths constitute a strategic reality, why the Church treats vows as sacraments and the law treats perjury as blasphemy\u2019s cousin \u2014 and why the breaking of the first great covenant could birth a god-sized wound. Mechanically this changes nothing: no new rules, no new subsystems. It is the flavor layer that makes every class, spell, and social contract in 5e feel native to this world rather than imported into it."));
children.push(H2("Divine Magic and the Sanction"));
children.push(P("Divine magic flows from the Matron \u2014 genuinely. Clerics, paladins, and divine casters channel her power exactly as the rules describe, and the power is real regardless of what any institution rules about it. What the Church of the Lupine Matron claims is not the source but the license: the Sanction, its asserted sole authority to ordain who may lawfully channel, interpret, or minister. A sanctioned cleric carries a pewter warrant-medal; an unsanctioned healer in Church lands is a theological problem that the Office of Omens exists, in large part, to solve. The See of Orlath issues its own rival sanction \u2014 which means the fractured empire has two licensing authorities for the same living goddess, and she has conspicuously declined to rule on the dispute: both churches\u2019 clerics keep their spells. The DM-facing joke is quiet and total: the Matron answers everyone who calls sincerely, and the institutions have built two empires of paperwork on top of her silence."));
children.push(H2("Chartered Thaumaturgy \u2014 the Arcane"));
children.push(P("The academy already treats magic as \u201Ca specialized skill like any other\u201D (canon), and this pass extends that to the empire\u2019s whole arcane culture: wizardry is chartered, credentialed, and bureaucratized wonder \u2014 the Byzantine relationship to engineering, applied to the impossible. A Chartered Thaumaturge of the House of the Craft holds a license enumerating the schools they may practice and the wards they may raise; the Charter is prestige, employability, and legal cover in one document. Outside the charter system live the hedge-mages \u2014 village workers of small practical magic, tolerated exactly as long as nothing goes wrong \u2014 and Rivergate\u2019s gray market in unlicensed scrollwork, where the penalty structure has taught practitioners to be very good or very quick. Sorcerers (\u201Cthe Kindled\u201D) sit awkwardly across all categories: power without study reads to the chartered as cheating and to the Church as a question, and bloodline fire in a world of covenant-metaphysics invites the obvious whisper \u2014 whose old bargain are you carrying?"));
children.push(H2("Warlocks: the Sworn"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "A pact is an oath, and a warlock is a person who swore one to something that answered. In this setting that makes warlocks theologically radioactive in Church lands \u2014 not because their power is necessarily evil, but because an unexamined covenant is precisely the category of thing this world\u2019s buried history proves can end civilizations. The Office of Omens keeps a standing file classification for suspected pact-bearers. Velmareth, characteristically, keeps a fee schedule instead. (" }, DM("DM note: "), { t: "warlock patrons should be built carefully in this setting \u2014 every patron relationship is a live wire running near the campaign\u2019s central mechanism, and at least one patron option, developed later with sign-off, could knowingly or unknowingly trace back to the thing beneath Aenodira.)" }]));
children.push(H2("The Old Observance"));
children.push(P("Druids and rangers practice the faith\u2019s pre-imperial stratum \u2014 the Matron as she was worshipped before there was an empire to sanction anything: field-shrines, first-fruits, the wolf-watch on winter roads. The Suthmark\u2019s harvest processions and planting-calendar saints\u2019 days (canon) are the domesticated descendant of this; in Tarnovar\u2019s Old Wood and the Ardven fells it survives undomesticated. The Church\u2019s position on the Old Observance is studied non-comment: it cannot condemn practices older than its own doctrine without inviting questions about what else predates doctrine \u2014 a nervousness the DM knows is better-founded than the Synod does."));
children.push(H2("Scarred Ground"));
children.push(P("Magic is scarred where great betrayals happened. Wards will not anchor at the Cold Door (canon); the same principle generalizes gently across the world: sites of oath-breaking at scale \u2014 massacre-fields under parley signs, the Weld, the deeps beneath the Old Forum \u2014 resist beneficial magic, sour divination, and hold cold. Practitioners call such places thin-written. No mechanical subsystem: adjudicate as environmental flavor with occasional concrete effects (disadvantage on a ritual here, a divination returning grief instead of answers there), always as story."));
children.push(H2("The She-Wolf of the Night Sky"));
children.push(P("The Matron\u2019s iconography is lunar and nocturnal: the She-Wolf of the Night Sky, the moon her watching eye, the stars her pack, dawn her gift rather than her element. The coronation prayer\u2019s address to the night sky is theologically precise, not poetic \u2014 the great liturgies are sung at night, and the Drow are her first-favored children of the dark. Dawn services exist and are beloved, but every Zhuvedian knows in their bones which direction prayer faces after sundown: up."));
children.push(H2("The Packlaw: Gender and Authority"));
children.push(P("A pattern runs through the empire\u2019s seats of settled power that Zhuvedian doctrine names outright rather than leaving unremarked: Empress Nyreeza ruled in her own right, not as regent; Duchess Vasq, Voivode Morn, Matriarch Corvane, Saint-Regent Olvesa, Legate Thorne, and Mistress Shen hold their offices by the same unquestioned legitimacy a man would. The Church calls this the Packlaw \u2014 teaching that the Matron leads Her pack as the wisest wolf leads hers, by right of endurance and judgment rather than raw strength, and that a pack\u2019s survival runs through its mothers as often as its sires. It bars no man from rule \u2014 Qilvayas sits the Lupine Throne \u2014 but it means nobody in the Crownlands remarks on a woman holding a duchy, a see, or an empire, any more than a person in our world remarks on a man holding one. The Imperial Cult\u2019s doctrine that the ruling bloodline carries Zhuvedus\u2019s divine spark sits easily beside it: the spark passes through blood, not sex."));
children.push(P("The doctrine runs strongest wherever authority is settled \u2014 thrones, duchies, sees, the machinery of a functioning institution \u2014 because settled authority is what the pack already recognizes. Authority still being fought for skews male throughout the fractured territories not by any law but by the same logic in reverse: a bastard never legitimized, a king awaiting a coronation not yet held, a raider refused a title four times, a warden whose authority his own Voivode has never sanctioned. A pack does not hand its trust to what hasn\u2019t yet proven it belongs. Once claimed and settled, the Packlaw would apply to these powers precisely as it does to the Crownlands \u2014 a detail their courts have not yet had cause to consider."));
children.push(H2("The Wolf-Shape: Lycanthropy and Doctrine"));
children.push(P("In an empire whose goddess wears the wolf, lycanthropy cannot be neutral \u2014 and is not. Church doctrine holds the wolf-shape taken by curse to be profanation, the Matron\u2019s image stolen and worn wrongly, and the Church hunts it with grim formality (the wolf-trial is a recognized proceeding, with recognized abuses). Folk belief, older and quieter, holds that sometimes the shape is her mark \u2014 and that the difference is visible in what the changed one does under the moon. Between doctrine and folk belief runs the setting\u2019s built-in gothic engine: the falsely accused, the genuinely monstrous, and the rare, terrifying possibility of a true-marked one, about which the Church\u2019s files are sealed and the Old Observance says nothing at all, loudly."));

children.push(H2("Marriage as Oath, and the Denmother\u2019s Choice"));
children.push(P("A wedding vow is the single most common binding oath an ordinary Zhuvedian will ever speak, and it carries the same literal weight as any other. Marriage is sworn before a Church witness in the Crownlands and loyalist provinces, or at the standing stones in Tarnovar, consistent with Tarnovari oath-custom generally. An unrepaired infidelity or abandonment is not merely private sorrow \u2014 unhealed, it is a small, personal echo of Zhuvedus\u2019s own crime, whether or not either party has ever heard the founding myth. Divorce exists, and, consistent with how the empire treats broken oaths everywhere else, it is not a document but a counter-oath: a formal Release, requiring Church sanction and mutual consent, or, on proven grave breach, a magistrate or Church tribunal may grant it unilaterally. A marriage ended without Release carries the same taint as any other unrepaired vow; one ended properly carries none."));
children.push(P("Titled succession follows the Packlaw\u2019s logic rather than strict primogeniture: a ruling parent formally names their heir by open declaration \u2014 the Denmother\u2019s Choice \u2014 revisable over the parent\u2019s lifetime, and it need not fall to the eldest, or even to a child by blood, in rare and dramatic cases. It is the naming that carries legal force, not the birth order. Duchess Vasq\u2019s court runs on this fact: with Aldous the elder, sickly, and Tavian the younger, ambitious, her succession is a live and unresolved question rather than a formality \u2014 which is precisely why the Garland needs to be as good as it is. Qilvayas himself has named no heir of either kind, a fact his court is certainly aware of and does not discuss above a whisper. Set against the Packlaw\u2019s confidence sits a quieter fact the court keeps quiet. For a line as long-lived as the Founder\u2019s Blood, its emperors have not lasted: within living memory the throne has turned over faster than a Drow reign should end, twice passing to heirs too young to rule and the regents who ruled for them. Each turn carries its own settled explanation \u2014 a fever, a faction, a border gone wrong \u2014 and each is plausible, and no one at court sets them side by side. Nyreeza, ruling in her own right to a son old enough to follow her without a regent, was the first steadiness the throne had known in generations; her disappearance ended it. That is the weight beneath the whisper that Qilvayas has named no heir. An heir is a small and mortal thing to hang an empire on, in a family that has not managed to keep its rulers."));
children.push(P("Regional variation is light but real. Tarnovar extends its existing custom \u2014 a dead Tarnovari\u2019s oaths, not their body, are what the living formally inherit, spoken at the standing stones by whoever takes up the unfinished promise \u2014 to leadership itself: a title passes to whoever takes up the stone, which is very often a named heir but does not have to be. The Suthmark times common inheritance to the harvest calendar, the same rhythm as its burials, so estates settle at a season\u2019s turn rather than the moment of death. Elsewhere, ordinary property is governed by local custom rather than fixed imperial law."));
children.push(H2("Language and Literacy"));
children.push(P("Old Imperial and Common divide along a written and spoken line, not a regional one, and Old Imperial is now fully dead as anyone\u2019s native tongue \u2014 no community, however remote, still dreams in it. It survives purely by function, kept alive where the empire\u2019s oldest institutions still need it: the Golden Tablets and the Zhuvedian Laws as drafted, the Church\u2019s rites and the Office of Omens\u2019 formal writs, proceedings at Highcourt, and the Academy\u2019s core instruction. Common is what the empire actually speaks, in every province from the Crownlands to the Brekelands, and nobody is born to anything else."));
children.push(P("Literacy in Old Imperial tracks education and office, not province or blood \u2014 which means it cuts across the empire sideways, in a way region never does. A Farrowgate dock laborer and an Ostmark legion sergeant are equally fluent in spoken Common and equally unable to read a magistrate\u2019s writ; what separates either of them from Archjurist Vhal is Academy admission, clerical training, or a guild apprenticeship that happened to include letters, not where either was born. The party \u2014 Academy finalists, fluent and literate by training across all three houses \u2014 moves through a mostly unlettered world almost without noticing the advantage, which is worth surfacing at the table in an NPC\u2019s deference rather than stating outright."));
children.push(P("The regional texture follows naturally from what\u2019s already established. Ardven\u2019s subsidized literacy is not simple generosity \u2014 it is Karvel\u2019s argument made visible, that his restoration delivers what Aenodira\u2019s has only promised, and it only reads as an argument because the empire\u2019s own baseline is genuinely worse. Tarnovar inverts the hierarchy entirely: a culture that swears on standing stones treats the written word as the lesser evidence, not the greater, since a spoken oath is witnessed and binding the instant it leaves the mouth, while a written contract is what people resort to when they don\u2019t trust each other enough to swear properly. Book Two\u2019s innovation \u2014 that an oath binds at law only if sworn before a recognized witness \u2014 is something Tarnovar has practiced by custom for longer than the empire has had a Book to write it in, and a Tarnovari envoy who says as much to an excited Aenodiran clerk isn\u2019t being rude; she\u2019s stating a fact of her own upbringing."));
children.push(P("The law pass sharpens the stakes considerably. Books One through Four run entirely on witness, mark, and register \u2014 which means the Laws\u2019 actual protection reaches only as far as a person\u2019s access to someone who can read and write on their behalf. An unwitnessed, unrecorded arrangement isn\u2019t illegal under Book One, only unenforceable, and the people least likely to find a recorded witness are, disproportionately, the people least able to read what they\u2019d be agreeing to in the first place. It is the campaign\u2019s central mechanism in miniature: the silence around a broken word doesn\u2019t require malice, only a gap nobody thought to close. Rivergate\u2019s document underworld \u2014 Mosse\u2019s dead seals, the Inkhands\u2019 arrangements, a district that trades in provenance rather than fabrication \u2014 isn\u2019t new invention against this backdrop. It\u2019s what a for-hire literacy economy looks like once someone thinks to name it."));
children.push(PS([{ t: "How this framework maps onto the Marked is left deliberately open here, reserved for the dedicated personhood pass \u2014 Book One\u2019s own unresolved clause deserves its full weight, not a footnote borrowed from someone else\u2019s gap.", i: true }]));

children.push(H2("The Willing Shape: Wildshape and Doctrine"));
children.push(P("Wildshape is not lycanthropy, and Church doctrine \u2014 for once \u2014 agrees without much argument. A druid\u2019s wolf-shape is willed, self-possessed, and takes nothing from anyone; there is no theft of the Matron\u2019s image to punish, because nothing was stolen. The wolf-trial has no jurisdiction here, and no sanctioned Church court has ever convened one against a druid for the shape itself. Folk belief goes further and warmer than doctrine bothers to: in the provinces, and especially where the Old Observance still runs strong, a druid who wears the wolf is read as walking proof of favor, and villages that would never touch a stray wolf for luck will happily touch the hem of a druid who has just changed back."));
children.push(P("The Office of Omens, characteristically, finds a third position: not persecution, but paperwork. A Chartered practitioner\u2019s licensing file quietly notes wildshape capability the way it notes any other unusual talent, and a druid who takes wolf-shape often or publicly enough to be noticed can expect a polite, entirely non-threatening visit from someone with a ledger, asking careful questions about frequency, circumstance, and whether anything unusual happens under the moon. Nothing comes of it, usually. The Office simply likes to know where the Matron\u2019s shape is currently walking around \u2014 and reserves the right to be very interested indeed if it ever hears the word \u201Cunusual\u201D twice about the same person."));
children.push(H2("The Wolf-Price: Blood Debts and the Provinces"));
children.push(P("A wolf\u2019s death by human hand carries two separate reckonings, and only one of them is the law\u2019s. Imperial statute \u2014 reformed under Qilvayas to say plainly what older law only implied \u2014 recognizes self-defense as a full defense against a wolf-killing charge, provided the killing is reported to a magistrate within three days and the body produced or accounted for. Get that right, in a place with a magistrate worth the name, and the law is satisfied."));
children.push(P("The law was never the whole of it. Older than any statute is the Wolf-Price \u2014 a debt owed not to the throne but to the wolf itself, or to whatever stands in for it: the nearest shrine-keeper, the local almonry, sometimes an old woman in a village with no shrine at all who has simply always been the one people pay. The price is small and never negotiable \u2014 coin, meat, a season\u2019s labor, whatever the keeper judges fits the death \u2014 and paying it has nothing to do with guilt or innocence. A traveler who kills a wolf defending a child pays exactly the same price as one who kills carelessly; the debt isn\u2019t about fault, it\u2019s about balance. What changes with the circumstances is reputation: pay the price promptly and the story that follows out of town is \u201Cthey did right by it.\u201D Delay, argue, or leave before the shrine-keeper is satisfied, and the story that follows is worse than anything a magistrate could write down \u2014 and in places far enough from a garrison, the magistrate\u2019s paperwork was never going to be what protected you anyway."));
children.push(H2("The Chosen Beast: Companions and the Befriended"));
children.push(P("A wolf that chooses a person \u2014 a ranger\u2019s companion, a freed animal that stays, a pack that walks a traveler\u2019s road unbidden \u2014 is read across the empire as the Matron\u2019s visible favor, and it functions like minor rank: doors open, elders nod, clergy will bless the animal (and quietly take note of the person). Doctrine distinguishes sharply between the befriended and the tamed. A wolf that stays of its own will honors the Matron; a wolf collared, caged, leashed, or sold profanes her \u2014 a wolf-cage is scandal verging on sacrilege, and no honest market in the empire sells one. Feeding wild wolves is lawful and, at the empire\u2019s margins, encouraged (Orlath\u2019s pilgrim stations are the famous case); the custom everywhere else is superstitious care \u2014 you feed the pack at the treeline, and you never call them in."));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Mechanically nothing changes \u2014 but the mapping matters: the Beast Master ranger is where core 5e grants a true permanent companion, and a Beast Master whose bond is a wolf is the standing, sacred-animal version of everything above \u2014 not a temporary shape but a lasting relationship with the Matron\u2019s own beast, which provincial villages read as being touched and capital institutions keep on file. Druids, by contrast, get no permanent companion in core rules; wildshape is the druid\u2019s entire animal relationship, and doctrine treats it accordingly (see The Willing Shape). Familiars and animal handling work as written. Socially, a visible wolf companion is standing \u2014 warmer reactions among the devout, harder scrutiny from the Office of Omens, exactly the wildshape grammar (a wolf-shaped familiar, being a spirit wearing her shape, is doctrinally fascinating and reliably file-worthy). " }, DM("DM only, and quietly load-bearing: "), { t: "wolves will not abide thin-written ground. A companion wolf is, functionally, a living Wardstone \u2014 it will balk at scarred places before any instrument reads them \u2014 and no befriended wolf, however loyal, will enter the Old Forum district. Its person will never know why. The DM will." }]));
children.push(H2("Relics and the Blessed Economy (Approved Frame)"));
children.push(P("Per the approved fork: arcane items are scarce and chartered, but relics and blessed items flow through the faith. The Church\u2019s almonries sell sanctioned mercy at honest prices \u2014 stamped healing potions brewed under the Sanction, holy water, blessed tapers \u2014 and the great relic-treasuries hold the setting\u2019s serious magic: the femur of a saint inside a mace-head, a battle-standard that has never fallen, a reliquary that quiets fever in a room. Mechanically, relics are official magic items wearing provenance \u2014 a +1 mace is the Ferrule of Saint Oswenna, and the provenance is the point. The schism doubles everything: Orlath maintains a rival relic-treasury and disputes the authenticity of half the Sanctum\u2019s inventory, Rivergate runs a brisk trade in false relics (and, very occasionally, terrifyingly real ones with forged papers), and the Office of Omens polices the whole mess. Player-facing consequence: healing is purchasable and human-scaled; wonder is rare, holy, and always has a story attached."));


children.push(H2("Currency: The Zhuven"));
children.push(P("The empire\u2019s standard gold coin is the Zhuven \u2014 struck at Aenodira, Zhuvedus\u2019s profile on the obverse and the She-Wolf on the reverse, plural Zhuvens. It\u2019s the coin\u2019s formal name: what a magistrate enters in a ledger, what the Exchange\u2019s counting-houses call it, what appears in any written instrument. Almost nobody speaks it aloud. In Common, the coin is simply a wolf \u2014 \u201cthree wolves for a night\u2019s lodging,\u201d \u201ca wolf a day, all found\u201d \u2014 slang so old and so universal that most speakers have long since stopped hearing the metal in it at all. It\u2019s a small, exact echo of the split described above (see Language and Literacy): the lettered name a thing by what\u2019s written on it, and everyone else names it by what they can plainly see."));
children.push(P("Smaller denominations get considerably less ceremony: a silver strand and a copper mote, restruck by whichever provincial mint most recently had working dies, with no particular consistency of design or weight beyond what a scale will bear out. Table pricing stays the familiar gp/sp/cp abstraction throughout \u2014 the Zhuven, strand, and mote are simply what those coins are actually called and look like in hand."));
children.push(P("Not every hand that takes a Zhuven cares what\u2019s stamped on it. Velmareth\u2019s counting-houses weigh gold rather than read it, and take a Zhuven exactly as readily as any other coin of the same fineness \u2014 characteristically mercantile indifference to whose face buys the bread. Duke Norr doesn\u2019t melt down the Zhuvens his conquests bring him; he restrikes them, his own seal punched directly over the wolf, which costs him nothing and says everything the rest of his administration already says by other means. In Tarnovar, a Zhuven spends the same as anywhere, but the old proverb holds it at arm\u2019s length regardless: \u201ca stone remembers, a coin forgets\u201d \u2014 coin is for strangers, and a given word is worth more than money between anyone who matters."));

children.push(H2("Medicine and Disease"));
children.push(P("Healing in the empire runs on the same three-tier certification Book Three already governs, though nobody usually says it that way. Divine healing moves through the Church\u2019s Sanction \u2014 stamped potions, laying-on of hands, a warrant-medaled cleric at an almonry \u2014 expensive, guaranteed, and genuinely magical. Arcane healing sits under the Charter, scarcer and stranger, and mostly beside the point for an ordinary complaint. The third tier, mundane physic, falls to the Crown and its guilds the same as any other trade: Academy-trained physicians and chirurgeons, guild-certified, competent, and concentrated almost entirely in the cities and among people who can pay guild rates for years of someone\u2019s training. All three are real, licensed, and largely irrelevant to how most of the empire actually gets treated."));
children.push(P("Below every certified tier \u2014 the overwhelming majority of care, delivered to the overwhelming majority of people \u2014 sits the same uncertified layer that already covers hedge-magic and the shrine-keeper\u2019s Wolf-Price: a herbwife, a bonesetter, a midwife, the person in a village who has simply always been the one people go to. None of it is illegal \u2014 nobody built a licensing apparatus for it any more than anyone licenses a mother setting a child\u2019s fever, and the guilds have never troubled to claim jurisdiction over care too poor to be worth regulating. It is also the only medicine most of the empire will ever see, which means its quality is a matter of local reputation and nothing else: genuinely skilled in one village, genuinely useless in the next, and nobody keeping a register either way."));
children.push(P("Farrowgate sharpens the whole picture into an actual crisis rather than an abstraction. The district\u2019s overcrowded, unplanned housing along the Long Wall\u2019s inner face breeds a chronic, recurring lung-sickness residents call the Damp \u2014 nothing exotic, nothing shadow-touched, just what happens when too many displaced people are packed into rooms with too little light and worse drainage, generation after generation of refugees arriving to find the last generation\u2019s problem still unsolved. A stamped Potion of Healing cures an active case as completely and instantly as it closes a sword-wound; Church magic doesn\u2019t discriminate by the dignity of the ailment. What it can\u2019t do is fix a drainage ditch, and no almonry has ever claimed jurisdiction over municipal works. The Damp isn\u2019t a medical failure \u2014 it\u2019s a reach problem, exactly like the Brekelands\u2019 unenforced law against chattel slavery: the empire\u2019s principles are sound, its presence doesn\u2019t extend to the people who need them, and that gap is where the real damage happens."));
children.push(P("Elsewhere the causes vary more than the pattern does. Velmareth\u2019s reed-sea breeds its own recurring fever \u2014 the marsh-ague, blamed locally on bad air off the channels rather than the mosquitoes that actually carry it, though the Compact\u2019s physicians have begun to suspect otherwise and haven\u2019t yet found anyone with the authority to act on the suspicion. Rivergate, characteristically, trades in the gap between the two: patent tonics and miracle cure-alls sold under forged Sanction marks or invented guild credentials, Book Four\u2019s forgery statute notwithstanding, because a bad batch of physic gets a customer\u2019s own judgment blamed long before anyone thinks to blame the seller. Rivergate discounts everything including the truth, and medicine is no exception."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "keep the Damp, and disease generally, mundane by design \u2014 same ruling as Greywell. Not every hardship in a fractured empire is the shadow\u2019s doing, and a party that starts reading every fever as metaplot loses the thing that makes the real metaplot land when it finally shows up." }]));

// ==================== PART II: PEOPLES ====================
children.push(H1("Peoples of the Empire"));
children.push(P("The empire is human-majority \u2014 right for its Byzantine texture \u2014 with the other peoples distributed where history put them. Distribution below; regional detail in Part V."));
children.push(B("The Founder\u2019s Blood (Drow):", "Zhuvedus was Drow \u2014 the Matron\u2019s chosen, first-favored child of her night \u2014 and the imperial dynasty is his surviving line. Threnvos was Drow as well (approved): twin champions of the same goddess, the same blood, one throne. There is no Underdark and no spider-cult in this world; Drow are simply the old blood of the founding, and they are nearly gone \u2014 a scattering of ancient noble houses claiming distant dynastic kinship, and the throne itself. The founder\u2019s people dwindling to a single family on a single chair is the campaign\u2019s decline theme in miniature. A Drow character outside the dynasty is therefore an enormous statement (Session Zero\u2019s existing note, now with teeth): they will be read as dynastic kin, dynastic claimant, or something nobody has words for. DELIBERATELY OPEN: whether any line of Threnvos survives. If it does, it is Drow, it is rightful-claimant material of the highest order, and it should not be spent early \u2014 same handling class as the Tarnovar revelation."));
children.push(B("Humans:", "The overwhelming majority everywhere, in named regional cultures: Zhuvedians (Crownlands core), Ostfolk, Suthfolk, Tarnovari, Brekelanders, Velmarenes, Orlathines, Ardvenners, the Skell, Normerines. Human cultural texture per region in Part V."));
children.push(B("Dwarves:", "The Stonesworn of Tarnovar\u2019s highlands \u2014 the stone-oath culture and dwarven nature are a natural marriage, and roughly a third of Tarnovar is dwarven, fully Tarnovari in identity. Smaller populations: the Suthmark\u2019s eastern hills (including the villages around Greywell, which is part of why the missing girls stay missing \u2014 hill-dwarf families have learned the valley\u2019s law does not climb), Normere\u2019s hired stonewrights, and the capital\u2019s masons\u2019 guild. Archivist Vell is a hill dwarf (which quietly explains a career that has outlasted four chancellors)."));
children.push(B("Elves:", "Wood elves keep to the deep forests \u2014 Tarnovar\u2019s Old Wood and the Ardven wilds \u2014 as the long-memoried folk of the Old Observance; canon-safe on the founding secret (2,000 years exceeds any living memory), but elven ballad-memory reaches closer to the fracture than human record does, which makes old elves valuable and dangerous listeners. High elf blood persists in a handful of faded Crownlands houses \u2014 more portraits than people now. Half-elves are the empire\u2019s courtier class (Chancellor Sorral, canon) and Velmareth\u2019s natural children."));
children.push(B("Half-Orcs:", "Legion stock \u2014 respected veteran families of the Ostmark marches, descendants of frontier auxiliaries who earned the rolls the hard way. Brune Halloc of the Inkhands is one of Rivergate\u2019s. No orc \u2018menace\u2019 exists in this setting; the marches\u2019 old auxiliary peoples were absorbed, not fought."));
children.push(B("Halflings:", "The river-trade people \u2014 Coppergate\u2019s copyists and landlords, the Ostrun barge families, Velmareth\u2019s middle guilds. Comfortable, networked, and collectively the best-informed civilians in the empire."));
children.push(B("Gnomes:", "The House of the Craft\u2019s natural constituency \u2014 instrument-makers, ward-wrights, archival engineers. Tirell Mosse, dealer in defunct authorities, is a rock gnome (of course he is)."));
children.push(B("Tieflings \u2014 the Marked:", "In a world of covenant-metaphysics, tieflings are what an ancestor\u2019s bad bargain looks like three generations later: bloodlines carrying a covenant made flesh. Feared in Church lands, filed by the Office, tolerated in Velmareth and Rivergate where everything is tolerated at the right rate. A Marked character\u2019s line-covenant is a ready-made personal hook \u2014 and, handled with sign-off, one such line could eventually trace somewhere campaign-relevant."));
children.push(B("Dragonborn \u2014 the Sarkanni:", "Rare famous foreigners: mercenary companies from across the southern sea, known by reputation (superb, expensive, contract-scrupulous \u2014 a Sarkanni company has never broken terms in living memory, which in THIS setting is remarkable and remarked upon). Available for PCs; minimal footprint otherwise."));
children.push(B("The Wolves:", "Sacred empire-wide. Killing a wolf is terrible luck, worse law, and \u2014 in the provinces \u2014 grounds for the kind of trouble that magistrates decline to interrupt. Wolves are omen-bearers: their behavior is read the way other cultures read birds. (The DM knows the wolves of Aenodira have been leaving the Old Forum district, one family at a time, for three years.)"));



// Founding Myth
children.push(H1("The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath"));
children.push(H2("Three Accounts of the Founder"));
children.push(P("No single account of Zhuvedus\u2019s nature is universally accepted. Three distinct traditions coexist, each serving different interests, and each believing itself the truth."));
children.push(B("Church Doctrine (Public):", "The Church of the Lupine Matron teaches that Zhuvedus was a mortal hero, chosen and favored by the Matron but never divine himself \u2014 virtuous in his triumphs, flawed in his failures, and mortal in his death. To the Church, only the Matron is properly divine; elevating any mortal, however great, to godhood would be heretical. \u201CSinful children of Zhuvedus\u201D is meant in this light: heirs to a great but flawed man, not to a god."));
children.push(B("The Imperial Cult (Public):", "A smaller, older priesthood tied to the imperial household teaches otherwise \u2014 that Zhuvedus was elevated to divine or semi-divine status upon his death, and that the ruling bloodline carries a literal spark of his apotheosis. This doctrine underwrites the throne\u2019s claim to hereditary legitimacy: only Zhuvedus\u2019s line, in this telling, has the right to rule. The Church tolerates this cult\u2019s existence because confronting it directly would mean confronting the throne itself."));
children.push(B("The Buried Truth (Hidden):", "A far older, deliberately suppressed tradition holds a darker and more specific account \u2014 that Zhuvedus was not the Matron\u2019s only champion in his own era, that his rise involved the destruction of a rival she had also favored, and that his triumph came at the cost of a sacred oath broken in her own witness. This is the account developed below. It survives only in fragments: old ballads the Church has worked for generations to lose, half-remembered oral traditions in the further provinces, and the private knowledge of a very few people still living \u2014 among them, Vaelindra."));
children.push(H2("Threnvos and the Sworn Covenant"));
children.push(P("Long before the empire existed in anything like its current form, the Matron favored more than one champion in a single generation. Alongside Zhuvedus stood Threnvos, leader of a different people with an equal claim to her blessing. Rather than see one destroy the other, she is said to have bound them under a sworn covenant \u2014 witnessed and sanctified by her own power \u2014 some form of joint rule, mutual restraint, or shared founding. The exact terms are lost. What survives is only that it was sacred, that both men swore it freely, and that it was meant to hold."));
children.push(P("Zhuvedus broke it. He destroyed Threnvos and claimed sole right to found what became the empire, and he did it under the eyes of the god who had sanctified the promise between them."));
children.push(H2("The Oathbreaker\u2019s Crown"));
children.push(P("Zhuvedus was, by the account preserved in the buried tradition, a paladin \u2014 sworn to the Oath of Devotion, a champion whose given word was meant to be inviolable. Breaking a sacred oath sworn before a god does not simply end quietly; it corrupts. In shattering his covenant with Threnvos, Zhuvedus fell from his oath and rose again as something else \u2014 an Oathbreaker, drawing on darker power in the same moment he used it to destroy his rival and seize the founding of the empire alone."));
children.push(P("This is, perhaps, the empire\u2019s deepest and most carefully guarded irony: the throne whose current occupant is rewriting the Golden Tablets in pursuit of lawful restoration was founded by a broken oath, and the man who broke it drew the very power of his triumph from that betrayal. Neither the Church nor the imperial cult preserves this detail \u2014 both have every reason not to. A paladin or cleric with sufficient insight standing in the right place, however, might still recognize the particular wrongness of Oathbreaker corruption for what it is."));
children.push(H2("The Shadow\u2019s Origin (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("Threnvos did not simply die. Whatever he felt in that final moment \u2014 betrayal, rage, the specific grief of being killed by a man he\u2019d trusted enough to swear brotherhood with \u2014 did not dissipate with him. It became the seed around which the wound left by the broken oath took shape. What waits in the vision\u2019s storm clouds is not a two-thousand-year-old grudge, and it is not a mindless force of pure entropy \u2014 it is both, fused together at the moment of its birth. The personal grievance gave the impersonal hunger a shape and a direction; the impersonal hunger has kept the personal grievance fed and growing long after any ordinary vengeful spirit would have faded."));
children.push(P("That fusion is what makes the entity dangerous in the present tense. Every oath broken since \u2014 every noble house that abandoned fealty, every treaty a warlord tore up, every governor who quietly stopped honoring the crown \u2014 has fed something that already had a will and a memory attached to it. It has been growing more itself, not just larger, for two centuries of imperial fracture. This gives player and NPC choices around loyalty, betrayal, and broken promises a direct, if invisible, bearing on the threat shown in the opening vision \u2014 a mechanism the DM can use quietly throughout the campaign without ever stating it outright to the players."));
children.push(P("The binding site \u2014 where the oath was broken and Threnvos died \u2014 lies beneath the imperial capital itself, beneath the Old Forum (confirmed; see Geography and Locations), which explains why the vision shows that specific city burning rather than some other place of significance. This ties the site\u2019s development directly to the capital\u2019s geography (see Geography and Locations) and gives Empress Nyreeza\u2019s disappearance a concrete shape: she was very likely searching for evidence of this true founding account, and quite possibly found the site itself."));

children.push(H2("The Turning Away (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("The founding was witnessed. Several hundred people stood in the Witness Hall beneath what is now the Old Forum to see Zhuvedus and Threnvos swear the covenant that would make an empire \u2014 and when Zhuvedus broke it, the assembly turned away in unison. In this world\u2019s metaphysics that is not cowardice but mechanism: oaths bind because they are witnessed, which is why the Tarnovari swear on stone before their kin, why marriage requires a Church witness, and why Duke Norr has every landholder swear before the assembled hall. When the assembly refused to see, they unwitnessed the covenant, and the unbinding is what made the betrayal possible rather than merely unpunished. Zhuvedus struck the blow. The witnesses made it a founding."));
children.push(P("Two consequences follow, and they govern the campaign. First, the shadow does not feed only on broken oaths but on the silence around them \u2014 every institutional look-away in the empire is the same act as the Hall, from a duchess letting a false record stand to a chancellor who asks exactly one question and never a second. Second, witnessing starves it, and the Vigil is the counter-rite: watched, lit, never left alone, owed to the dead as dead and not as the good. The Vigil is penance. It was instituted by the witnesses or their children as an apology none of their descendants remember making, which is why it is the one practice that survives unchanged across every schism in the empire."));
children.push(P("The Golden Tablets were composed by the witnesses. The empire\u2019s sacred founding law is the self-exoneration document of the people who looked away \u2014 which is precisely what Empress Nyreeza meant when she wrote that the wound predates the Wall and the Tablets were written over it. The law was the cover-up, written first. Emperor Qilvayas is rewriting the Tablets now, and a legal code that makes oaths enforceable, witnessed, and recorded is, in this metaphysics, a starvation regimen against the thing beneath his city. He has built the only real weapon by institutional instinct, without any idea what he is doing. His Daily Draw \u2014 the habit his clerks find inefficient \u2014 is the Emperor personally witnessing one citizen a day, and it is the most important thing he does."));
children.push(H2("The Scouring, and Emperor Vaskaren (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("Roughly two hundred years ago, as the fracture began, Emperor Vaskaren the Restorer went looking for why his empire was failing. He found the Witness Hall, and he had it erased \u2014 methodically, professionally, by masons working for weeks under a commission paid from the imperial purse with its purpose left blank. The second crime is the same crime: an act of un-witnessing performed by the one family the founding had made. Vaskaren\u2019s restoration failed. Qilvayas is the second restorer of that line, and does not know whether he is repeating the pattern or breaking it."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only \u2014 The Throne\u2019s Short Reigns: "), { t: "The court\u2019s mundane explanations for the turnover are sincere, and may be the whole of it \u2014 a declining empire is hard on the families that rule it, and long-lived lines die of ordinary causes like any other. Whether a longer pattern runs beneath the ordinary ones is left deliberately open, the way the Matron\u2019s presence is: do not resolve it at the table, and do not hand a later DM the answer here. It is enough that Zhuvedus\u2019s line broke the covenant, buried the evidence, and scoured it a second time under Vaskaren \u2014 and that the family which did those things has never since held its throne in peace. Whether that is guilt, coincidence, or appetite is a door the campaign keeps ajar. If it is ever opened, it is opened at the table, by the players, and not before." }]));
children.push(P("The scourers missed one section, forty feet up on the eastern gallery\u2019s highest tier, where a man on a scaffold worked alone at the end of a long day. Two spans of frieze survive: dozens of faces carved with the particularity of portraiture, house-sigils worked into collars, every one of them turned away in the same instant with the composure of people who have decided together and without a word to see nothing. Several of those houses hold seats at court today. Vaskaren\u2019s order survives in the dynastic archive; it is how Nyreeza found the way down, and it is what waits, unopened, in her sealed dispatch case."));
children.push(H2("The Unkept Vigil (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("Threnvos was killed by his sworn brother under the Matron\u2019s own witness and buried under a wall, under a forum, under a founding myth \u2014 with no Vigil, no watching, no light, and nobody to see him home. The wound beneath Aenodira is an unkept vigil two thousand years long. The entity has two fused components: Threnvos\u2019s grief, which is a person and can be laid to rest, and the impersonal hunger the broken covenant unleashed, which is neither. Keeping the Vigil separates them \u2014 a real victory and not the victory. The grief goes quiet because it was the only part a funeral could touch; what remains does not grieve, does not remember, and cannot be reasoned with, because the part that could have been reasoned with is the part laid to rest."));
children.push(P("This is the campaign\u2019s pivot from a dungeon problem to a civilization problem. What remains is fed by every broken word and every averted eye in the fractured empire, and it will keep eating as long as that continues. There is no monster to kill at the bottom of the hole. There is a civilization to fix \u2014 which makes the Zhuvedian Laws the actual weapon and the Grey-Gold Rising considerably more than a set piece. On the Matron: she may be down there, and may always have been, keeping the vigil nobody else would. Divine magic functions normally in the deep reach and clerics who pray receive, but there is never a form, never a voice, and never a confirmation. The ambiguity is permanent and deliberate."));

// Qilvayas
children.push(H1("Emperor Qilvayas and the Lupine Throne"));
children.push(H2("The Young Emperor"));
children.push(P("Qilvayas is a 110-year-old Drow \u2014 young by the standards of his long-lived race, but old enough to rule without a regent. He ascended to the Lupine Throne following the mysterious disappearance of his mother, Empress Nyreeza. Raised within the imperial palace, he received the finest education available in history, law, military strategy, theology, and statecraft. More importantly, he studied the empire\u2019s decline intimately, tracing its causes and contemplating solutions."));
children.push(P("His youth gives him energy and idealism, but also makes him somewhat untested. He has never commanded armies in the field, never navigated the brutal realities of imperial politics at the highest level, and never faced the compromises that restoring an empire might demand. His vision is clear, his ambition genuine, and his belief in the righteousness of his cause unshakeable \u2014 but the question remains whether he can adapt that vision to reality without losing himself in the process."));
children.push(H2("The Man Behind the Crown"));
children.push(P("In person, Qilvayas is precise, courteous, and quietly relentless. He speaks softly and in the cadences of law \u2014 clauses, provisos, careful definitions \u2014 and he never raises his voice, having learned early that an emperor who shouts has already lost the room. He works brutal hours, sleeps little even by Drow standards, and keeps three habits his court has learned to read: he walks the Long Course alone at dawn twice a week (guarded at a distance, to the Palatine Guard\u2019s despair); he answers petitions from commoners personally, one each day, chosen at random from the pile (his clerks call it the Daily Draw and privately think it inefficient; he considers it the point); and he has never, in three years, entered his mother\u2019s study. It is cleaned weekly, by his order. Nothing in it may be moved."));
children.push(P("His grief for his mother is real, unresolved, and armored over with work. Known to almost no one: among Nyreeza\u2019s effects is a sealed dispatch case, her personal cipher on the lock, which Qilvayas has never opened \u2014 officially because the succession inventory has not reached it, actually because he is afraid of what it contains. It sits in the sealed study. If the party ever earns his trust deeply enough, or forces the issue, the case is a campaign-scale revelation waiting in a locked room."));
children.push(H2("The Court"));
children.push(B("Lord Chamberlain Vareth Kessin:", "Gatekeeper of access (see Geography and Locations, Highcourt). Serves the office more than the man; served Nyreeza first, and his opinion of how the son compares to the mother is a thing he has never once expressed, which everyone finds more unnerving than any stated view."));
children.push(B("Archjurist Senna Vhal:", "Head of the Law Commission drafting the Zhuvedian Laws \u2014 brilliant, exhausted, and the person who actually holds the pen on the empire\u2019s new foundation. She fights a running quiet battle with Kessin over access to the Emperor and a louder one with the provinces over every clause. If the restoration has a second-most-important person, it is her, and she knows exactly how expendable that makes her."));
children.push(B("Legate Bruvasca Thorne:", "Commander of the Palatine Guard (see Geography and Locations, the Garrison) \u2014 and, by any honest reckoning, the finest field commander the throne possesses. Her name was made at the Ashline, where she broke a Skellvard raid with eight hundred men against five times that number; the soldiers\u2019 songs about it are the closest thing the modern legions have to a victory anyone remembers. That is precisely her problem. The court needs her, the legions love her, and the throne can never quite decide whether her loyalty is a fact or a fortune it has not yet had to spend. She has been given every duty except the one she was made for: an army large enough to actually reconquer something. Her husband Bram, a retired quartermaster who manages her correspondence and is widely underestimated, keeps a private ledger of every promise the court has made her and quietly amends it after every audience. See The Powers of the Fractured Empire for her expanded profile and threads."));
children.push(B("Mistress Averil Shen:", "Keeper of what is formally the Bureau of Correspondence and functionally the throne\u2019s intelligence service \u2014 and Mistress by the precise style of a commoner who holds an imperial bureau in her own right, not by courtesy \u2014 small, underfunded, and run on Shen\u2019s personal network of clerks, couriers, and debts. She was Nyreeza\u2019s creature before she was Qilvayas\u2019s, and she is the only member of the court who conducted her own private inquiry into the Empress\u2019s disappearance. What she found, or failed to find, is hers to reveal."));
children.push(B("Hierophant Malzeth Corr:", "Head of the imperial cult (see The Keepers of the Ascent). Present at court for every rite and most councils; influential in exact proportion to how much the throne needs its divinity that season."));

children.push(H2("The Vision of Restoration"));
children.push(P("Qilvayas\u2019s plan for imperial restoration rests on several pillars:"));
children.push(B("Legal Reform:", "He seeks to rewrite the Golden Tablets, creating a new comprehensive legal code \u2014 the \u201CZhuvedian Laws\u201D \u2014 that will modernize governance, eliminate corruption, and provide a unifying framework for all imperial territories. In his own words, these laws will be \u201Cforged by the fires of justice and cast in molds of wisdom.\u201D This echoes the historical Corpus Juris Civilis of Emperor Justinian \u2014 the belief that law itself can be a civilizing and unifying force."));
children.push(B("Administrative Revival:", "He is rebuilding imperial institutions that have atrophied or disappeared. The Imperial Academy is a key part of this effort, training a new generation of loyal, competent administrators who understand his vision."));
children.push(B("Diplomatic Reunification:", "Where possible, Qilvayas prefers to bring fractured territories back into the fold through negotiation, offering stability, legal protection, and the prestige of the empire in exchange for renewed loyalty."));
children.push(B("Military Force When Necessary:", "While he prefers peaceful methods, Qilvayas is prepared to use force against those who threaten imperial stability or refuse reasonable terms. He understands that some warlords and breakaway states will never willingly submit."));
children.push(P("The question the campaign will explore is whether these idealistic goals can survive contact with reality \u2014 and what Qilvayas might become in the pursuit of restoration."));
children.push(P("That question has a mythic precedent he likely doesn\u2019t know he\u2019s repeating. The empire\u2019s own founder broke a sacred oath in pursuit of a goal he believed justified it, and became something else in the process (see The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath). Every pragmatic compromise Qilvayas makes in the name of restoration \u2014 every ally set aside, every promise expedient to break \u2014 walks the same road his ancestor walked to its end. Whether he ever recognizes the echo is one of the campaign\u2019s central open questions."));
children.push(H2("Law, Oath, and Bound Labor"));
children.push(P("A reforming Emperor modeling himself on the historical architect of the Corpus Juris Civilis inherits that architect\u2019s hardest question along with his ambition: what does the empire\u2019s law actually say about a person\u2019s claim on another person\u2019s labor? Zhuvedian law has a clean, old answer for the worst of it \u2014 chattel slavery is illegal and doctrinally blasphemous throughout the empire, and has been for centuries, on grounds that grow directly from the oath-magic itself: if a sworn word binds because the speaker\u2019s will is real, then owning a person outright, denying they possess a will capable of being bound, is theologically incoherent. The capacity to swear is the proof of personhood; the law does not entertain owning what can vow."));
children.push(P("Beneath that clean line sit three legal categories the coming Zhuvedian Laws will have to reconcile with each other. Oath-bound service \u2014 Duke Norr\u2019s entire system in Normere \u2014 is fully legal: a subject swears personal service directly, and the oath is real and binding because it was freely spoken, regardless of how much pressure made \u2018freely\u2019 a technicality. Debt-bondage and indenture are legal, regulated, and time-limited \u2014 an ordinary remedy for debt or minor infraction, bounded by contract, ending when the contract says it ends. Hostage-diplomacy, the Academy\u2019s own admission practice among them, is a wholly separate category and not bondage at all: a diplomatic guarantee between powers in which the hostage retains full legal personhood and is owed treatment as an honored, if constrained, guest \u2014 a standard high enough to explain the practice\u2019s respectability and loose enough that individual cases can still go quietly wrong."));
children.push(P("The empire\u2019s actual crisis is not its law but its reach. Imperial statute forbids chattel slavery everywhere; imperial statute has not reached everywhere in two hundred years. In the Brekelands, where warlord companies are, in plain terms, little better than bandits with armies, that gap is where the law\u2019s clean answer stops being an abstraction and starts being an atrocity nobody enforces against. It is, in the most literal sense available, exactly what Qilvayas\u2019s restoration is for."));
children.push(H2("The Mystery of Empress Nyreeza"));
children.push(P("Qilvayas\u2019s mother, Empress Nyreeza, disappeared under circumstances that remain unclear. She was the one who began revitalizing the Imperial Academy and took the first steps toward institutional reform. Her sudden absence created a power vacuum that Qilvayas filled, but it also left questions: Was her disappearance natural, political, or something more sinister? Did she flee, was she taken, or is she dead? Does Qilvayas know more than he admits?"));
children.push(P("Her fate remains a thread that can be pulled as the campaign develops \u2014 potentially revealing allies, enemies, conspiracies, or truths about the empire itself. One known thread already exists: the Seeress Vaelindra of the Still Waters was among the last people to meet with the Empress before her disappearance, and she has never spoken of what passed between them (see Key NPCs and Factions)."));
children.push(P("The working theory (see The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath) is that she was searching for evidence of the empire\u2019s true founding account, and quite possibly found the site where it happened. Whether that discovery left her dead, transformed, or simply trapped somewhere no one has thought to look is still undecided, and can remain a genuine mystery the party uncovers through play."));

// ==================== THE ZHUVEDIAN LAWS ====================
children.push(H1("The Zhuvedian Laws"));
children.push(P("Archjurist Senna Vhal\u2019s Law Commission is compiling the Zhuvedian Laws as seven Books, promulgated together this coming Solacre on the Long Course \u2014 the empire\u2019s first comprehensive legal code since the Golden Tablets, and Qilvayas\u2019s own described ambition: laws \u201cforged by the fires of justice and cast in molds of wisdom.\u201d Each Book governs one domain of imperial life; together they are meant to do for the fractured empire what the historical Corpus Juris Civilis did for Justinian\u2019s."));

children.push(H2("The Golden Tablets, in Fragment"));
children.push(P("The Tablets themselves do not survive complete \u2014 no document does, across two thousand years \u2014 but six lines are old enough and famous enough that every Seal-house student still has them by heart, carved over more than one provincial courthouse door:"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cBy the will of the Lupine Matron and the true voice of her chosen son, Zhuvedus, is this Empire raised, and these words set down that all who come after may know the order of things.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cNo free soul may be bound as chattel, for the wolf does not cage her cubs, and what can vow cannot be owned.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cLet every vow be kept, for a broken word is a wound upon the pack.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cHe who spills wolf-blood without cause shall answer to the pack\u2019s keeper, and the price shall be paid in full before the moon turns.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cThe Throne descends to whom the sitting wolf names before the pack, living or dying, and the pack shall know no other law of blood.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "\u201cSet down in the year of the founding, by those who stood witness to the covenant of the Throne, that the memory of that day never fade.\u201d", i: true }]));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "read against the new Laws, what the fragments never do is the point. The vow-clause never says how anyone would know a vow was made, by whom, or that it was broken \u2014 no witness, no record, no venue; Book Two exists to close exactly that silence, and Qilvayas has no idea why closing it matters. The wolf-clause is the \u201colder law only implied\u201d self-defense the Wolf-Price statute already references (see The Wolf-Price, above) \u2014 now with actual words, and they still never say \u201cself-defense,\u201d because the Tablets never define what excuses a wolf\u2019s death at all; they only set the price. And the colophon is the quiet one: the Tablets call their own authors \u201cthose who stood witness\u201d \u2014 true, and the worst possible word for what the Witness Hall\u2019s assembly actually did (see The Turning Away). Any Seal-house character who has known that line their whole life is, without realizing it, quoting the exact self-description of the people who un-witnessed a god\u2019s covenant. Never explain this at the table. Let a player notice it, or don\u2019t \u2014 either way it is already true the moment the frieze is found (Session Seven), and it lands harder for having been sitting in a courtroom inscription the whole campaign." }]));

children.push(H2("Book One \u2014 Of Persons"));
children.push(P("Book One codifies the empire\u2019s existing framework for a person\u2019s claim on another person\u2019s labor (see Law, Oath, and Bound Labor, above), unchanged in substance: chattel slavery remains illegal and blasphemous; oath-bound service, debt-bondage and indenture, and hostage-diplomacy remain the three legal categories beneath it. Book One adds one new requirement: to be enforceable in an imperial court, an oath-bound service agreement or a debt-bondage indenture must be sworn before a witness empowered to record it \u2014 a Church witness, a magistrate, or, in Tarnovar, a standing stone. An unwitnessed labor arrangement is not illegal. It is simply not enforceable: a master with no recorded oath has no imperial recourse if the bound party walks away. It is, in a fractured empire whose worst abuses cluster exactly where no one is watching, a narrow but real answer \u2014 and it costs the Commission nothing that wasn\u2019t already true of every other oath in Zhuvedian law."));
children.push(P("The legal personhood of the Marked remains, deliberately, Book One\u2019s one unresolved clause. Velmareth already grants tieflings full legal standing that no other jurisdiction extends (see Free City of Velmareth and the Delta Compact) \u2014 whether the Commission ever writes that standing into imperial law is a live question the Laws do not yet answer."));
children.push(P("The gap isn\u2019t that Zhuvedian law names the Marked as property \u2014 nothing does, and Book One\u2019s prohibition on chattel slavery is unconditional. The gap is that the law\u2019s actual protections depend on a Marked person\u2019s oath being recognized as an oath at all, and nothing in Book One or Book Two affirms that it is. A service agreement or an indenture is only as enforceable as its witness is willing to certify it, and a Church witness who privately doubts a tiefling\u2019s capacity to swear in the full theological sense can simply decline \u2014 leaving the exact same unwitnessed vulnerability the Laws were written to close, for exactly the people who can least afford it. The Office of Omens files the Marked the way it files a frequent wildshaper, except permanently rather than provisionally: a standing watch rather than a triggered one."));
children.push(P("The doctrinal argument runs both ways, and runs sincerely both ways, which is why the Commission hasn\u2019t settled it. The exclusionary case borrows its caution directly from how the Church already treats warlocks: a pact is an oath that answered, and a bloodline visibly carrying an ancestor\u2019s answered bargain is a permanent, hereditary record of unfinished business with whatever wasn\u2019t the Matron. But the empire\u2019s own founding legal principle cuts the other way with equal force: if the capacity to swear is what proves personhood, a tiefling is the single most legally unambiguous case in the empire \u2014 proof carried on the body that an oath was sworn and something held. Vhal is understood to lean toward writing Velmareth\u2019s standard into Book One outright before promulgation; whether she has the votes, or the political room left this close to Solacre, is not yet decided even inside her own Commission."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "this is deliberately live, not deliberately open \u2014 unlike Nyreeza\u2019s fate or the throne\u2019s short reigns, this one is built to be decided, and decided by the party. Whatever they bring to bear before the Solacre promulgation \u2014 testimony, an alliance, evidence, a favor called in with Vhal or against her opposition \u2014 can tip Book One\u2019s final language either way. If the campaign reaches Solacre without the party engaging the question at all, the Commission settles it without them, off-screen, by whichever way the political weather was already blowing; the DM should not manufacture urgency the party hasn\u2019t created for itself." }]));

children.push(H2("Book Two \u2014 Of Oaths and the Witness"));
children.push(P("Book Two states plainly what the empire\u2019s oldest customs have always assumed: a sworn word binds because it is witnessed. Marriage before a Church witness, Norr\u2019s personal oaths sworn before his assembled halls, the standing stones of Tarnovar \u2014 all already comply without a clause needing to change. What Book Two adds is procedural: perjury, a false oath sworn before a recognized witness, is a distinct offense from ordinary lying, tried in whatever venue the oath itself was sworn in. Its penalty is never a fine. It is entered against the liar\u2019s own signet-record (see Book Four) \u2014 a mark that follows the person rather than a debt that can be paid off, which is precisely why Zhuvedian doctrine has always called perjury blasphemy\u2019s cousin rather than mere fraud."));

children.push(H2("Book Three \u2014 Of the Sanction and the Charter"));
children.push(P("Book Three gives formal shape to certification across all three of the empire\u2019s licensing bodies \u2014 the Church\u2019s Sanction over divine goods and practice, the House of the Craft\u2019s Charter over arcane goods and practice, and the Crown and guilds over mundane trade, tolls, and weights and measures. In every domain, certification is two things, never one: a physical mark \u2014 a wax stamp, a pewter warrant-medal, a charter seal, a guild-stamp \u2014 and a matching written entry in the certifying body\u2019s own register: a parish or almonry ledger, a Chartered Scriptorium\u2019s roll, a guild\u2019s books. The mark alone proves nothing to a suspicious buyer or a sitting magistrate; what actually settles a dispute is whether the register entry exists and matches."));
children.push(P("Possessing unstamped goods or practicing without a license is not, by itself, a crime \u2014 it shifts the burden. A magistrate, a Church tribunal, or a Charter inspector may demand the seller or practitioner produce their register entry on formal challenge, and it is the failure to produce one, not the missing stamp itself, that opens a case. This is the calculation Rivergate\u2019s gray market already prices into its discounts: the goods are usually genuine, and the risk being sold at a markdown is the registration, not the quality."));

children.push(H2("Book Four \u2014 Of Seals and Record"));
children.push(P("Book Four names forging an imperial instrument \u2014 a seal, a signet, a writ, a chartered license \u2014 as an offense in its own right, distinct from whatever fraud the forgery enables: restitution plus a term of indenture (Book One\u2019s second tier) scaled to the value of what was falsely authorized, escalating to exile from the offending jurisdiction on a second offense. Every chartered license, and every signet issued for identification, is dual-recorded exactly as Book Three requires: the physical mark, and an entry in the issuing body\u2019s own register. Copying wax is easy. Copying a Chartered Scriptorium\u2019s register, an almonry\u2019s ledger, or an academy\u2019s roll is a fundamentally harder crime \u2014 which is exactly why Rivergate deals in provenance rather than fabrication."));
children.push(P("An office that ceases to exist must surrender its seal formally to the Imperial Archive before its authority lapses; only after surrender may the retired instrument change hands as a curio. It is a small, dry clause, and it is the entire reason a trade like Tirell Mosse\u2019s \u2014 dealer in the seals, signets, and letterheads of forty defunct institutions \u2014 is a legitimate one and not a smuggling operation."));

children.push(H2("Book Five \u2014 Of the Wolf"));
children.push(P("Book Five is the shortest of the seven, because it changes nothing: it simply gives the existing Wolf-Price statute (see The Wolf-Price: Blood Debts and the Provinces, above) formal standing among the Laws rather than leaving it to stand alone. Self-defense remains a full defense against a wolf-killing charge, reported to a magistrate within three days with the body produced or accounted for; the older, non-legal debt to the nearest shrine-keeper remains exactly as it was."));

children.push(H2("Book Six \u2014 Of the March"));
children.push(P("Book Six is military law, narrow by design: every subject owes the empire service in some form, deliberately undefined in scope (see The Vision of Restoration, above); border-lord and warlord private forces are tolerated as a fact of the fracture rather than licensed; and an officer who defrauds his own command is triable by court-martial at Legate level, appealable once to Aenodira \u2014 the exact mechanism Magistrate Ondrei\u2019s complaint against Colonel Dessen has been waiting on."));
children.push(P("The Book also gives the legions\u2019 chain of command formal shape for the first time:"));
children.push(table(["Rank","Command","Body"],[26, 36, 38],[
  ["Legionary","Self","Imperial Legions"],
  ["Sergeant","~10 (a file)","Any regular force"],
  ["Centurion","~80\u2013100 (a company)","Imperial Legions"],
  ["Colonel","A garrison or single legion","Imperial Legions"],
  ["Legate","Multiple legions, or the capital\u2019s standing force; answers directly to the Throne","Imperial Legions / Palatine Guard"],
  ["Censor / Censor-Captain","A detachment of Office Examiners","Office of Omens\u2019 military arm"]
]));
children.push(P("Marshal is not a rung on this ladder. It is an acclamation the Throne grants a field commander for extraordinary personal renown, entirely apart from the size of the force under him \u2014 which is why Gavric Dane, who commands a single march\u2019s garrison, is styled Marshal while Legate Thorne, who commands the Palatine Guard itself, is not. Dane holds a Colonel\u2019s actual authority and a Marshal\u2019s public weight, and the gap between the two is precisely what makes the court\u2019s quiet anxiety about him a structural fact rather than an unexplained rumor."));
children.push(P("Provincial and warlord forces carry no standard rank of their own; \u201cCaptain\u201d serves empire-wide as a courtesy title for anyone commanding a company-sized irregular force \u2014 a border lord\u2019s retinue, a warlord company, a Sarkanni mercenary contract-officer \u2014 without implying any place in the legions\u2019 ladder at all."));

children.push(H2("Book Seven \u2014 Of Judgment"));
children.push(P("Book Seven answers the question every provincial town already answers in practice without a name for it. There is no separate civilian watch in the Crownlands or the loyalist provinces: order-keeping is a standing duty of whatever legion or garrison detachment is posted locally, and in Aenodira itself, of the Palatine Guard. A magistrate \u2014 judge and civil administrator combined in a single office, as Cassivar Ondrei already is in Dravenna \u2014 receives complaints, hears testimony (a witnessed oath carries real evidentiary weight under Book Two; a false one is perjury, not merely a bad witness), and rules without a jury. Sentencing runs the Laws\u2019 own ranges: fines, restitution, indenture under Book One, rarely worse."));
children.push(P("Appeal to Aenodira exists, and Book Seven does nothing to make it fast \u2014 Ondrei\u2019s complaint against Colonel Dessen is itself the proof, still sitting in Legate Thorne\u2019s inbox years on. Secular justice and the Church\u2019s own process (the Office of Omens\u2019 escalating Writ of Examination) run in parallel and can conflict; a sufficient political favor can have a writ quashed as obstruction of an open imperial inquiry, but only as an exceptional intervention from above, never as a routine appeal."));
children.push(P("Outside the loyalist provinces, in thin country, there is often no resident magistrate at all, and disputes fall to whoever locally holds informal authority \u2014 a village elder, or the same shrine-keeper who already judges the Wolf-Price, called on for grievances that have nothing to do with wolves. In the Brekelands, and anywhere warlord-held, there is no magistrate and no appeal: the warlord or his captains are judge, jury, and enforcement, full stop."));

// Titles, Ranks, and Precedence
children.push(H1("Titles, Ranks, and Precedence"));
children.push(P("The empire has never kept a single order of precedence, and the omission is not an oversight. Two centuries of fracture left the Lupine Throne granting titles it can no longer enforce and recognizing titles it never granted, so that a Voivode and a Duke are not two rungs of one ladder but the heads of two different kinds of thing. What follows is five ladders, each running to its own summit, and one honest rule for what happens where they cross."));

children.push(H2("The Throne and the Court"));
children.push(P("Precedence at court is by access. This is the first thing a provincial learns in Aenodira and the last thing he believes: rank on paper decides where a man stands at a rite, but who reaches the Emperor decides everything else \u2014 and the man who decides who reaches the Emperor commands no soldiers, holds no land, and is one of the most powerful people in the empire."));
children.push(table(["Style", "Office", "Held by"], [26, 40, 34], [
  ["Emperor / Empress", "The Lupine Throne", "Qilvayas; Nyreeza, missing three years"],
  ["Lord Chamberlain", "Keeper of access to the Emperor\u2019s person", "Vareth Kessin"],
  ["Archjurist", "Head of the Law Commission drafting the Zhuvedian Laws", "Senna Vhal"],
  ["Mistress / Master", "The style of a commoner who heads an imperial bureau", "Averil Shen, Keeper of the Bureau of Correspondence"],
  ["Magistrate", "Judge and civil administrator in one office (Book Seven)", "Cassivar Ondrei of Dravenna"]
]));
children.push(P("Master and Mistress are not courtesies but a precise legal style: they mark a commoner holding an imperial bureau in her own right, as against the noble officers of the household. Averil Shen is Mistress Shen because she is not a lady and has never once pretended to be. The Bureau is hers by appointment and competence, and the style says so."));

children.push(H2("The Legions"));
children.push(P("The one ladder the empire has already written down is the legions\u2019, set out in Book Six \u2014 Of the March, above: Legionary, Sergeant, Centurion, Colonel, Legate. Marshal stands outside that ladder as an acclamation of the Throne, and Captain serves empire-wide as a courtesy for anyone commanding an irregular company. Book Six is the authority; nothing here amends it."));

children.push(H2("The Church of the Lupine Matron"));
children.push(table(["Style", "Authority"], [28, 72], [
  ["Matriarch", "Voice of the Matron in Aenodira; the Church\u2019s most senior public figure"],
  ["Synod of the Grey", "Council of senior clergy; advises the Matriarch, and constrains her"],
  ["Prelate", "Head of a Church office of jurisdiction \u2014 Sarvin Odell, the Office of Omens"],
  ["Hierophant", "Head of the Keepers of the Ascent \u2014 the imperial cult, a parallel body and not Church clergy"]
]));
children.push(P("The Church has no single supreme authority, and this is a decision rather than a vacancy. The Matriarchate is collegial: the Matron\u2019s Voice speaks for the faith in the capital and is checked at every turn by the Synod of the Grey, which exists precisely so that no one throat holds the whole of it. A Prelate therefore holds his jurisdiction from the Synod and not from the Matriarch personally \u2014 her colleague with his own writ, not her subordinate. This is why the Office of Omens can pursue a policy the Matriarch has not endorsed and cannot simply countermand, and why anyone hoping to appeal over Prelate Odell\u2019s head will spend a long and instructive season discovering there is no head to appeal over."));

children.push(H2("The Imperial Academy"));
children.push(table(["Style", "Role"], [26, 74], [
  ["Chancellor", "Head of the Academy \u2014 Emeth Sorral"],
  ["Professor", "Senior faculty, often holding a named charge \u2014 Corvin Dail, Master of Trials"],
  ["Professor Emeritus", "Retired faculty; the style is kept for life, whatever the circumstances of departure"],
  ["Instructor", "Junior faculty \u2014 Liria Fenn, House of the Craft"],
  ["Archivist", "Keeper of the restricted stacks \u2014 Dathenor Vell"]
]));

children.push(H2("Sovereign and Border Styles"));
children.push(P("These do not rank against one another, and none of them ranks beneath the Throne except where the Throne can make it so. Each style records what kind of power it is."));
children.push(table(["Style", "What it means", "Held by"], [23, 47, 30], [
  ["King", "A crown claiming full sovereignty", "Karvel of Ardven"],
  ["Sea-King", "King of a people rather than a territory: no land, no seat, no grave", "Aldrec the Landless"],
  ["Voivode", "Tarnovar\u2019s war-leader raised to sovereign; absolute within her word", "Ysavet Morn"],
  ["Ban", "A march-warden holding a frontier for a Voivode, with wide latitude and little supervision", "Dregan Morn, the Eastmarch"],
  ["Duke / Duchess", "An imperial ducal house \u2014 a title the Throne granted, whether or not it is still obeyed", "Garvin Norr of Normere; the Suthmark house"],
  ["Count / Countess", "A landed noble seated beneath a ducal house", "Velsanna Ory of Greywell"],
  ["Lord", "Any landholder with retainers and no better title", "Ostrev, on the eastern road"],
  ["Saint-Regent", "Unique to Olvesa: the See\u2019s regency of the True Rite", "Olvesa the Reconciled"],
  ["Harborlord", "One of Velmareth\u2019s ruling merchant-bankers", "the Meldane house among them"],
  ["Guildmaster", "Head of a chartered trade body", "Ptolan Vess, Capital Merchants\u2019 Concord"]
]));
children.push(P("The distinction that matters most is the one nobody says aloud. Duke Norr and Voivode Morn are not peers of unequal rank; they hold different kinds of title entirely. Norr\u2019s is imperial \u2014 granted by the Lupine Throne, which makes him on paper the Emperor\u2019s own subject, conducting a rival restoration inside the empire\u2019s own law. Morn\u2019s is foreign, and owes Aenodira nothing whatsoever. That asymmetry is the reason Normere is the more dangerous of the two: a Voivode who defies the Throne is a neighbor, while a Duke who does it is a precedent."));

children.push(H2("When the Ladders Meet"));
children.push(P("A Prelate, a Legate, and a Duchess share no scale at all, and they meet constantly. The empire\u2019s practical answer is unwritten and none the weaker for it: precedence at court is by access, precedence in the provinces is by force, and precedence in a shrine is by rite. What happens when those three disagree has never been set down. The Zhuvedian Laws, which have an opinion about grain weights and the wolf-price alike, are conspicuously silent here \u2014 and the silence is not something Archjurist Vhal has overlooked. It is the one question she has been told not to answer, because answering it would oblige the Throne to say out loud whose empire this is."));

children.push(H2("Forms of Address"));
children.push(table(["Speaking to", "Say"], [38, 62], [
  ["The Emperor", "Your Radiance, in form; Majesty, in the shorthand of a court that uses it daily"],
  ["A King, Voivode, or Sea-King", "Your Grace"],
  ["A Duke, Duchess, or Ban", "Your Grace"],
  ["A Count, Countess, or Lord", "My lord; my lady"],
  ["The Matriarch", "Your Voice"],
  ["A Prelate or Hierophant", "Your Reverence"],
  ["Saint-Regent Olvesa", "Mother \u2014 the See uses nothing grander, at her insistence"],
  ["A Legate or Marshal", "Legate; Marshal. The legions do not embroider"],
  ["A Magistrate", "Your Honor"],
  ["Academy faculty", "Professor; Instructor"]
]));
children.push(P("Getting one of these wrong is not a crime and rarely even an insult. Getting one deliberately wrong \u2014 calling Olvesa Your Holiness, or naming Dregan Ban of the Fence to his face \u2014 is a sentence with a knife in it, and every soul in the room will hear it land."));

// Academy
children.push(H1("The Imperial Academy of the Lupine Throne"));
children.push(H2("Purpose and History"));
children.push(P("The Imperial Academy was originally founded during the empire\u2019s golden age to train the administrators, officers, and specialists needed to govern and defend such a vast realm. Like much else, it fell into decline as the empire fractured \u2014 many regional academies closed, curricula degraded, and standards slipped."));
children.push(P("Empress Nyreeza began the academy\u2019s revival in the years before her disappearance, recognizing that rebuilding imperial institutions required trained personnel. Emperor Qilvayas has continued and expanded this effort, seeing the academy as essential to his restoration plans. He needs loyal, competent people who understand his vision and can implement his reforms across the empire."));
children.push(P("Today, multiple Imperial Academies exist across territories that still recognize the throne, but the Imperial Academy of the Lupine Throne in the capital remains the most prestigious. Studying at the capital academy means access to the best instructors, the imperial library and archives, proximity to power, and the possibility of advancement in imperial service. Graduation from the capital academy opens doors that provincial schools cannot match."));
children.push(H2("Admission and Student Body"));
children.push(P("The empire maintains a policy of universal education \u2014 in theory, any subject can seek admission to an Imperial Academy. In practice, admission to the capital academy operates through a lottery system that is less fair than it appears:"));
children.push(B("The Official Process:", "Applicants from across the empire enter their names into an annual lottery for admission. The system is designed to give opportunity to talented individuals regardless of birth or wealth."));
children.push(B("The Reality:", "Wealthy families make generous \u201Cdonations\u201D to the academy, and their children mysteriously get selected. Local lords ensure their favored prot\u00E9g\u00E9s gain admission. Some slots are reserved for hostage-diplomacy situations \u2014 sons and daughters of border lords and ambitious nobles kept close to ensure good behavior. But enough legitimate lottery winners make it through that the system maintains its legitimacy and the student body remains genuinely diverse."));
children.push(P("This creates a fascinating mix: children of privilege alongside scholarship students who earned their place, hostages resentful of being pawns alongside true believers in the empire, ambitious commoners seeking advancement alongside nobility maintaining family traditions. The academy deliberately fosters this diversity, believing that future imperial servants need to understand and work with people from all backgrounds."));

children.push(H2("The Three Houses"));
children.push(P("The Imperial Academy is organized into three houses, each focusing on different areas of study necessary for imperial service. Students affiliate with a primary house based on their chosen career path but regularly cross-train in the other houses as their profession requires."));
children.push(H3("House of the Sword"));
children.push(P("The House of the Sword trains officers, strategists, and those who will serve in martial or military capacities. The curriculum emphasizes not just combat skills but the art of leading, organizing, and sustaining military forces."));
children.push(B("Core Studies:", "Military tactics and strategy, logistics and supply chain management, leadership and command, military history and analysis of historical campaigns, fortification and siege warfare, cavalry and infantry doctrine, naval operations (where relevant)."));
children.push(B("Practical Training:", "Physical conditioning, weapons training, small unit tactics, command exercises with actual troops, war games and simulations."));
children.push(B("Typical Career Paths:", "Imperial legion officers, garrison commanders, military advisors to provincial governors, personal guards to nobility, mercenary company leaders, military engineers (with Craft cross-training), battlefield chaplains (with Seal cross-training)."));
children.push(H3("House of the Seal"));
children.push(P("The House of the Seal focuses on administration, law, theology, and governance \u2014 the civilian machinery that keeps an empire functioning. These students will become the magistrates, tax collectors, diplomats, and priests who actually run imperial territories day-to-day."));
children.push(B("Core Studies:", "Imperial law and the Zhuvedian legal codes, theology and doctrine of the Lupine Matron, rhetoric and persuasion, diplomacy and negotiation, taxation and fiscal policy, record-keeping and bureaucratic procedure, history and philosophy of governance."));
children.push(B("Practical Training:", "Mock trials and legal proceedings, diplomatic simulations, public speaking and debate, religious ceremonies and rituals, administrative case studies."));
children.push(B("Typical Career Paths:", "Magistrates and judges, imperial bureaucrats and administrators, tax collectors and treasury officials, diplomats and ambassadors, priests and religious officials, legal scholars, court advisors, intelligence officers (with Craft cross-training)."));
children.push(H3("House of the Craft"));
children.push(P("The House of the Craft is the most diverse and internally varied, encompassing anyone whose skills serve the empire but don\u2019t fit neatly into military command or civil administration. The unifying principle is practical application \u2014 these are people who make things, solve problems, and get specialized work done."));
children.push(B("Core Studies (varies by specialization):", "Engineering and architecture, medicine and surgery, alchemy and herbalism, cartography and surveying, languages and linguistics, codes and ciphers, arcane theory and spellcraft, artifice and magical item creation, trade and economics, arts and propaganda."));
children.push(B("The Role of Magic:", "Most arcane and divine casters at the academy study in House of the Craft, as magic is treated as another specialized skill to be mastered and applied. Wizards study spellcraft and magical theory, clerics and paladins study theology (though they may take courses in the Seal as well), druids study natural philosophy, and artificers combine magical and mundane engineering."));
children.push(B("Practical Training:", "Hands-on projects relevant to specialization, apprenticeships with master craftsmen or mages, field exercises applying skills in real situations."));
children.push(B("Typical Career Paths:", "Imperial engineers and architects, court physicians and healers, alchemists and apothecaries, cartographers and explorers, spies and intelligence operatives, war wizards (with Sword cross-training), trade negotiators, artists and cultural ambassadors, artificers and enchanters."));

children.push(H3("Class Placement by House"));
children.push(P("For character creation purposes, the thirteen base classes align with the houses as follows. Primary house reflects the class\u2019s core training; nearly every character concept will involve cross-training."));
children.push(B("House of the Sword:", "Barbarian, Fighter, Monk. Paladins may choose Sword as primary if their concept is a warrior shaped by faith rather than a religious figure trained for war."));
children.push(B("House of the Seal:", "Paladins who see themselves foremost as religious champions \u2014 think warrior-clergy in the Byzantine mold \u2014 may take Seal as primary with heavy Sword cross-training. Clerics with a doctrinal or pastoral focus may also justify Seal primary."));
children.push(B("House of the Craft:", "Artificer, Bard (arts and propaganda), Cleric (divine magic, with substantial Seal cross-training in theology), Druid (natural philosophy), Ranger (wilderness operations), Rogue (espionage, codes, intelligence), Sorcerer, Warlock, Wizard."));
children.push(P("The imbalance toward Craft is intentional \u2014 it is explicitly the catch-all house for specialists, and magic is institutionally treated as a specialized skill rather than a separate estate. Players should choose primary house based on character concept, with the paladin placement left deliberately flexible."));

children.push(H2("Cross-Training and Curriculum Structure"));
children.push(P("The academy\u2019s philosophy emphasizes cooperation between houses. The empire needs soldiers who understand law, administrators who respect military realities, and specialists who can work with both. To this end, cross-training is common and often required:"));
children.push(BUL(null, "A military supply officer studies primarily in the Sword (logistics, military operations) but takes extensive coursework in the Seal (procurement law, contracts) and Craft (engineering, trade)."));
children.push(BUL(null, "A battlefield medic is primarily Craft (medicine) with Sword secondary (understanding military operations, triage under fire)."));
children.push(BUL(null, "A combat engineer focuses on Craft (engineering, fortifications) with Sword training (siege warfare, military architecture)."));
children.push(BUL(null, "An intelligence officer might be Craft primary (espionage, languages, codes) with courses in both Seal (diplomacy, law) and Sword (military intelligence)."));
children.push(BUL(null, "A military chaplain studies Seal (theology, law) with Sword coursework (military hierarchy, morale, battlefield ministry)."));
children.push(P("This flexibility allows the academy to train highly specialized professionals while ensuring they can work effectively across disciplines \u2014 crucial for serving a complex empire."));

children.push(H2("Dormitories and Social Structure"));
children.push(P("Rather than segregating students by house, the academy deliberately assigns them to mixed residential halls based on year and cohort. This ensures that students from all three houses live, eat, and socialize together, breaking down professional silos and building the kind of cross-disciplinary relationships that effective imperial service requires."));
children.push(B("Dormitory Facilities:", "Each residential hall includes communal dining areas, study rooms and libraries, courtyards for exercise and sparring, small shrines to the Lupine Matron, common rooms for socializing and recreation."));
children.push(B("Social Dynamics:", "The dormitories are where lasting friendships form, where rivalries develop, and where students learn to see beyond house loyalties. Study groups mix houses, athletic competitions pit house against house for prestige, and social networks cross professional boundaries."));
children.push(P("Despite official encouragement of cooperation, house pride remains strong. Informal competitions, debates over which house is most essential to the empire, and good-natured (and sometimes not-so-good-natured) rivalries are part of academy culture."));

children.push(H2("Program Duration and Field Exercises"));
children.push(P("The standard academy program lasts four years. Students typically enter around age 18-20 (human equivalent) and graduate in their early twenties, ready to begin imperial service or pursue other paths."));
children.push(B("First and Second Years:", "Foundation coursework in primary house, introduction to cross-training, basic practical skills, intensive study and classroom work."));
children.push(B("Third Year:", "Advanced specialized study, deeper cross-training, more complex projects and simulations, beginning of practical application."));
children.push(B("Fourth Year (Final Year):", "Students complete supervised field exercises \u2014 real-world assignments that prove they can apply their training outside the safety of the academy. These might include serving with imperial legions on patrol, assisting magistrates in provincial courts, conducting surveys or diplomatic missions, solving problems for local authorities, or taking on adventuring contracts that serve imperial interests."));
children.push(P("Field exercises serve multiple purposes: they test competence under real conditions, build confidence and practical skills, create opportunities for students to distinguish themselves, and provide the empire with useful work from advanced students at minimal cost. Students often work in small mixed-house teams during these exercises, mirroring how they might operate in actual imperial service."));

children.push(H2("Post-Graduation Expectations"));
children.push(P("All subjects of the empire are expected to serve, though the definition of \u201Cservice\u201D is deliberately vague and enforcement uneven in the fractured realm:"));
children.push(B("Direct Imperial Service:", "Accepting commissions as officers, magistrates, engineers, or other official imperial positions. This is what the academy ideally produces."));
children.push(B("Regional Service:", "Returning home to serve local lords or territorial governments, technically fulfilling the service requirement without necessarily strengthening the central throne."));
children.push(B("Guild and Civic Service:", "Joining craftsmen\u2019s guilds, merchant associations, or other civic organizations that serve \u201Cthe empire\u201D in an abstract sense."));
children.push(B("Independent Operations:", "Striking out as adventurers, mercenaries, merchants, or specialists, often rationalizing that protecting imperial citizens and interests counts as service."));
children.push(B("Ignoring the Obligation:", "Some graduates simply pursue their own interests with no pretense of service, betting correctly that the weakened empire can\u2019t enforce compliance everywhere."));
children.push(P("The academy cannot and does not force specific career paths. Graduates make their own choices, though those who openly flout their obligations may find doors closed if they later seek imperial favor or positions."));

children.push(H2("The Proving (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("The capital academy maintains one tradition no student is ever told about: the Proving, a staged final examination administered irregularly, and only to cohorts the faculty judge exceptional. The chosen cohort receives what appears to be a genuine, dangerous commission \u2014 and completes it inside a controlled environment they believe is real, observed throughout by the faculty. The academy\u2019s reasoning is coldly sound: an examination a student knows is an examination measures preparation; only a test mistaken for reality measures judgment, mercy, leadership, and nerve. Graduates who pass are told at the conclusion, sworn to secrecy about the method, and marked in sealed records as Proven \u2014 a designation certain offices in Highcourt know to look for. The party\u2019s Proving is the subject of Sessions Three and Four (see the session module The Proving Below). The thematic rhyme \u2014 a benevolent institution deceiving its own for reasons it believes justify the deception, inside an empire founded on exactly that logic \u2014 is deliberate, and should never be stated aloud at the table. Let it sit."));

// Player Characters
children.push(H1("The Player Characters"));
children.push(H2("Who They Are"));
children.push(P("The player characters are final-year students at the Imperial Academy of the Lupine Throne, currently completing their field exercises. They come from diverse backgrounds \u2014 some from loyal imperial territories, others from breakaway regions or neutral ground. Some earned their admission through the lottery, others through family influence or political arrangement. They may have different primary houses and specializations, but their time together at the academy \u2014 sharing dormitories, working on cross-training projects, competing in house competitions \u2014 has forged them into a cohesive unit."));
children.push(P("They are competent but not yet famous \u2014 skilled enough to handle dangerous situations but still learning, still making mistakes, still figuring out who they are and what they believe. They represent the future Emperor Qilvayas is trying to build: capable, educated individuals who understand both the ideals of the empire and the messy reality of the fractured world."));
children.push(H2("Their Plans"));
children.push(P("The party has decided that after graduation, they will form their own adventuring company \u2014 a mercenary band operating under the principle that \u201Ca job is a job and coin is coin.\u201D This represents a choice to be masters of their own fate rather than following predetermined paths into imperial bureaucracy, military service, or guild positions."));
children.push(P("Their academy training makes them uniquely qualified for this work. They can handle diverse challenges \u2014 combat, negotiation, investigation, problem-solving \u2014 and their cross-training means they work together seamlessly despite different specializations. In the chaos of the fractured empire, there\u2019s always work for capable people willing to take on dangerous jobs that \u201Cdecent folk may not wish to risk themselves to do.\u201D"));
children.push(P("They might rationalize this as serving imperial interests (protecting imperial citizens, maintaining order, gathering intelligence) or simply be honest that they prefer freedom and profit over institutional service. Either way, they\u2019re planning to strike out independently once they complete their final exercises and officially graduate."));
children.push(P("But fate \u2014 or the gods, or something else \u2014 has other plans for them."));

// Inciting Incident
children.push(H1("The Inciting Incident: The Shared Vision"));
children.push(H2("The Vision Itself"));
children.push(P("After completing their latest field exercise and celebrating with the profits from their client, the party retires to their inn for the night. During sleep, each character receives the same vision \u2014 or perhaps nightmare:"));
children.push(P("Dark storm clouds gather over the imperial capital. The city below burns with infernal flames \u2014 not natural fire, but something wrong, something that shouldn\u2019t exist in the mortal world. The characters are present in the vision but powerless, unable to move or speak, only able to witness in horror."));
children.push(P("Their gaze is drawn upward to the storm clouds. Behind the roiling darkness lurks an immense shadow \u2014 not just large, but vast, world-shaking, wrong. It emanates a roar that resonates in their bones, that speaks of ancient power and terrible purpose. The characters cannot scream, cannot flee, cannot do anything but watch until the vision fades to blackness and they awaken drenched in sweat."));
children.push(P("When they compare notes, they realize with growing unease that they all experienced exactly the same vision, down to the smallest details. This is no ordinary dream."));
children.push(H2("The Response"));
children.push(P("Despite their academy training, the party has no frame of reference for this. Shared prophetic visions aren\u2019t part of the curriculum. But they know someone who might have answers: quiet word among academy scholars and capital contacts speaks of a Seeress in the city \u2014 a woman named Vaelindra, once of the Church, now independent \u2014 who is consulted discreetly by people of consequence when omens defy explanation. She is not advertised; she is referred. Finding her is the party\u2019s first step."));
children.push(P("The vision has marked them. Whether they want it or not, they are now connected to something larger than their plans for an independent mercenary company. The capital calls, and with it, their destiny."));
children.push(P("They must travel to the capital \u2014 back to the heart of the empire they thought they\u2019d left behind \u2014 and seek answers from the Seeress. What they learn there will determine not just their own futures, but perhaps the fate of the Empire of Zhuvedus itself."));

// Themes
children.push(H1("Campaign Themes and Potential Directions"));
children.push(H2("Core Themes"));
children.push(P("The Qilvayas Symphony explores several interconnected themes:"));
children.push(B("Legacy and Restoration:", "Can past glory be reclaimed? Should it be? What is lost in the attempt to restore what was?"));
children.push(B("Idealism vs. Reality:", "The tension between Qilvayas\u2019s noble vision and the compromises that achieving it might require. When does pragmatism become corruption? When does necessary force become tyranny?"));
children.push(B("Prophecy and Free Will:", "The party has seen a potential future. Can it be changed? Should it be? Are they agents of destiny or masters of their own fate?"));
children.push(B("Unity vs. Freedom:", "Is imperial reunification worth the cost to those who have built new lives in the fractured territories? Does order justify the loss of autonomy?"));
children.push(B("Power and Its Corruption:", "What happens when good intentions meet the brutal realities of power? How do individuals and institutions change when pursuing righteous goals through questionable means?"));
children.push(H2("Possible Interpretations of the Vision"));
children.push(P("The shadow creature in the vision can be developed in several directions, depending on the tone and focus of the campaign:"));
children.push(B("The Empire\u2019s Dark Reflection:", "The shadow represents what the empire becomes if Qilvayas\u2019s methods grow too brutal. The infernal flames are purges and forced compliance. The vision is a warning about the cost of restoration \u2014 showing the party what they might need to prevent or oppose."));
children.push(B("Ancient Power Unleashed:", "The Lupine Throne or some imperial regalia contains a bound entity or dark pact from the empire\u2019s founding. Qilvayas, desperate to save the empire, might reach for this power without understanding the consequences. The vision shows the result of that choice."));
children.push(B("External Existential Threat:", "The shadow is real, literal, and coming \u2014 an ancient enemy, a planar incursion, or something awakening. Only a unified empire has any chance of surviving, justifying Qilvayas\u2019s urgency but also forcing terrible choices about how quickly reunification must happen."));
children.push(B("Theological Crisis:", "The shadow represents corruption spreading through the faith of the Lupine Matron \u2014 heresy, false prophets, or something masquerading as divine will. The vision warns of spiritual catastrophe that could destroy the empire from within."));
children.push(B("Combination or Misdirection:", "The truth might combine elements from multiple interpretations, or the vision itself might be incomplete or intentionally misleading, sent by forces with their own agendas."));
children.push(P("Working answer for DM reference: the shadow is a fusion of the personal and the impersonal \u2014 Threnvos\u2019s dying essence bound to the force unleashed by Zhuvedus\u2019s broken oath, fed since by every betrayal the fractured empire has produced. See The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath for the full account. This should not be revealed to players outright; let them arrive at pieces of it through Vaelindra, Nyreeza\u2019s trail, and whatever they eventually find beneath the capital."));
children.push(H2("Potential Campaign Arcs"));
children.push(P("The campaign can develop in various directions based on player choices and interests:"));
children.push(B("Agents of the Empire:", "The party works with (or for) Qilvayas, helping implement his reforms, dealing with threats to reunification, and navigating the moral complexities of restoration. They become key players in imperial politics."));
children.push(B("Independent Investigators:", "The party pursues their original plan of independence but finds themselves repeatedly drawn into events connected to the vision. They operate in the spaces between factions, making their own choices about which side to support in any given situation."));
children.push(B("Opposition to Empire:", "If Qilvayas\u2019s methods become tyrannical or the vision reveals dark truths about imperial restoration, the party might actively work against reunification, supporting breakaway territories or seeking alternative solutions."));
children.push(B("Preventing Catastrophe:", "If the shadow represents an actual external or supernatural threat, the party might focus on investigating and preventing the catastrophe shown in their vision, regardless of political implications."));
children.push(P("The campaign\u2019s tone allows for moral complexity \u2014 there may be no clear \u201Cright\u201D answer to whether the empire should be restored, what methods are justified, or even whether the vision\u2019s warning can or should be heeded. The party will need to make difficult choices with imperfect information, and their decisions will have real consequences for the empire and its people."));

// Key NPCs
children.push(H1("Key NPCs and Factions"));
children.push(H2("Vaelindra of the Still Waters \u2014 The Seeress"));
children.push(B("Role in the Campaign:", "The first major NPC the party meaningfully interacts with. She validates the shared vision, reframes it as potential rather than prophecy, and becomes a recurring resource, moral compass, and eventual source of one of the campaign\u2019s most significant revelations."));
children.push(H3("Who She Is"));
children.push(P("Vaelindra is an elderly human woman \u2014 late seventies, perhaps older, difficult to tell \u2014 who has lived in the capital long enough to have watched three rulers come and go, including Empress Nyreeza. She was not always the Seeress. She arrived in the capital decades ago as a minor functionary in the Church of the Lupine Matron, bright and ambitious, until her first genuine vision destroyed her career. What she saw conflicted with Church interests; the hierarchy quietly sidelined her, and she has operated independently ever since \u2014 not quite official, not quite heretical, merely tolerated. Powerful people consult her quietly and would never admit it publicly."));
children.push(H3("Presentation and Manner"));
children.push(P("She is deliberately unmystical. No dramatic robes, no incense-filled chamber of mystery. She lives in a comfortable but modest apartment in a middle district of the capital, keeps meticulous written records of every vision she has ever interpreted, and receives visitors like a retired scholar receiving students. She is sharp, a little sardonic, and asks more questions than she answers. She has seen enough prophecy go wrong \u2014 interpreted incorrectly, weaponized politically, fulfilled in ways nobody anticipated \u2014 that she is deeply cautious about telling people what their visions \u201Cmean.\u201D"));
children.push(H3("Her Gift"));
children.push(P("Her gift is genuine. She receives true visions from time to time, and she has an uncanny ability to recognize patterns across different people\u2019s prophetic experiences. When the party describes their shared vision, she will recognize elements she has encountered before \u2014 not the same vision, but related imagery, similar symbolic language. This unsettles her deeply, because it suggests something much larger than a personal omen: something already in motion."));
children.push(H3("What She Tells the Party"));
children.push(P("She does not interpret the vision so much as confirm its legitimacy and share what she knows of its imagery. The shadow, the infernal fire, the burning capital \u2014 she has seen fragments of this before. She can tell the party the vision is real and connected to events already unfolding. But she pushes back firmly on the assumption that it is a prophecy of inevitable doom. In her experience, shared visions do not show what will happen \u2014 they show what could happen if nothing changes. This distinction preserves the party\u2019s agency: they are not spectators to a predetermined script but actors who may yet change the outcome."));
children.push(H3("Relationship with the Throne"));
children.push(P("Her most complicated entanglement is with the imperial family. Empress Nyreeza consulted her regularly, and Vaelindra was one of the last people to see the Empress before her disappearance. She has never spoken of that final meeting to anyone. Emperor Qilvayas knows she exists and knows his mother trusted her, but he has never sought her out \u2014 possibly because he fears what she might tell him."));
children.push(H3("Her Secret (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("Vaelindra\u2019s career-ending vision, decades ago, showed her fragments of the empire\u2019s true founding account \u2014 Zhuvedus, the broken oath, and the rival he destroyed to seize it (see The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath). That is precisely what the Church could not tolerate, and precisely why she was sidelined rather than elevated. She and Empress Nyreeza discussed this in their final meeting, and Vaelindra believes Nyreeza went looking for confirmation, or for the site itself, shortly before she vanished. Vaelindra also believes that Qilvayas\u2019s restoration project, however genuinely motivated, risks repeating his ancestor\u2019s exact error \u2014 that in his urgency to reunify the empire through law and order, he may yet break faith the way Zhuvedus once did, and accelerate the very conditions that lead to the burning capital of the vision. She has not decided what to do with this knowledge, or whether the party can be trusted with it. This revelation should be deployed at a dramatically appropriate moment mid-campaign, once the party has earned her trust \u2014 or forced her hand."));
children.push(H3("Resolved Design Notes"));
children.push(BUL(null, "How the party learns of her: resolved \u2014 Archivist Vell\u2019s referral (Session Two)."));
children.push(BUL(null, "Church posture: resolved \u2014 active conflict. The Office of Omens surveils her (the ground-floor copyist reports to the Office; she knows, and feeds it chaff) and moves to a Writ of Examination in Session Six."));
children.push(BUL(null, "Her knowledge depth: resolved by tier \u2014 she knows the shape (the founding was a burial; the wound lies beneath; Nyreeza sought it) but withholds names and specifics until earned. Her forced-hand partial revelation is Session Six; the full account remains a mid-campaign payoff."));
children.push(H2("The Powers of the Fractured Empire"));
children.push(P("Races, classes, and full stat blocks for the figures below appear in Appendices I\u2013II. The rulers, commanders, and monsters who define the territories of the Atlas. Each profile notes its historical key \u2014 the real figure whose life supplies its spine \u2014 for the DM\u2019s reference in improvising beyond what is written."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only \u2014 The Acceleration: "), { t: "One pattern connects these profiles, and no one on the board can see it. The shadow beneath Aenodira has been stirring since Empress Nyreeza reached the binding site three years ago \u2014 and its stirring leaks. Vaelindra\u2019s ledger records the visions quickening; Saint-Regent Olvesa\u2019s visions darkened the same season, which is why Orlath\u2019s coronation is suddenly imminent; the ancient green Vessarkath, still couched on the eagles she took sixty years past, chose that same season to let one leave her hoard, and the empire\u2019s Young Wolf was made downstream of it; the dreams that drive Ban Dregan\u2019s cruelty have worsened; the Skellvard\u2019s displacement, Norr\u2019s conquests, and the court\u2019s brittleness all sharpen in an empire growing subtly more faithless, more frightened, and more willing to break its word. The whole board is moving faster, and the players\u2019 campaign is the reason why. Never state this; let the tempo itself be the tell." }]));

children.push(H3("Sea-King Aldrec the Landless \u2014 Lord of the Skellvard Clans"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Alaric I. ", i: true }, { t: "Aldrec, in his fifties now, served the empire as a young man, and for twenty years after \u2014 Skell oarsmen and shock-fighters were cheap, and at the Weeping Strait, twenty years ago, an imperial admiral spent three thousand of them to save his own squadrons and logged it as victory. Aldrec survived, was denied the coastal command he had been promised, and went home to find the fjords already under pressure from the northern crowns as they then were; Karvel\u2019s consolidation of those crowns into Ardven, some five years later, would only finish what was begun. He has led the ship-clans ever since as a king without a country, raiding the coast of the empire he would rather serve. His demands have never changed: a coastal march to hold, a title to hold it by, and his people\u2019s names entered in the empire\u2019s rolls as subjects rather than vermin. The throne has refused him four times \u2014 each refusal recorded, like everything, on the tally-sticks his lawspeakers keep. Speech pattern: fluent, formal Imperial \u2014 better than most courtiers\u2019 \u2014 which unsettles people who expected a savage; he counts silently in Skell when angry." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Aldrec is the campaign\u2019s great persuadable \u2014 the enemy who wants to be an ally, whom the empire keeps manufacturing into a threat. If Qilvayas grants him his march, the coast gains its fiercest defender and the restoration its strangest proof of concept. If refused too long, Aldrec will do something vast and terrible that solves nothing \u2014 and he knows it, which is the tragedy. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "he is dying slowly of an old wound gone bad, and his succession is unsettled; the clans after Aldrec will not negotiate. The window he represents closes with him. Skell kings are buried under rivers, in secret; his greatest private fear is dying landless, with no river his people may safely turn." }]));

children.push(H3("Marshal Gavric Dane \u2014 the Young Wolf of the Ostmark"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Germanicus. ", i: true }, { t: "Gavric Dane is not yet thirty-five and already the most beloved soldier in the empire: the man who led the expedition that recovered the wolf-standard of the Ninth from the Brekelands, nearly sixty years after the Silvasse Disaster swallowed three legions and every banner they carried. He weeps openly at soldiers\u2019 funerals, remembers the names of privates\u2019 children, refuses gifts he cannot reciprocate, and has never once, in any recorded word, expressed an ambition beyond his command \u2014 which is precisely why half the court is certain he must be hiding one. The legions call him the Young Wolf. The court hears that name and thinks of succession. The Emperor has met him twice, decorated him twice, and both times been perfectly, studiously correct \u2014 two disciplined young men, each aware the other is watching. Speech pattern: warm, direct, remembers everyone; goes quiet rather than lies." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Dane is a loyalty test the campaign can pose to every faction, including the party \u2014 everyone wants to claim him, use him, or remove him. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the Piso gun on the mantel \u2014 if Dane ever dies suddenly, the empire will tear itself apart over who is to blame, and at least three factions (the court, Orlath\u2019s agents, Norr) could plausibly want it. Whether that gun ever fires is the DM\u2019s call; its existence should shadow every scene he is in. He has begun having bad dreams he tells no one about. He has never broken an oath in his life. The shadow has no purchase on him \u2014 yet \u2014 and something in the dark finds that interesting." }]));

children.push(H3("Ban Dregan Morn \u2014 Warden of the Eastmarch, Lord of the Fence"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Vlad III. ", i: true }, { t: "Kinsman to Voivode Ysavet Morn, sent to the capital academy as a hostage-student forty years ago under the admission system\u2019s quiet diplomacy, returned to Tarnovar with perfect Imperial manners and something broken behind the eyes. For thirty years he has held the Eastmarch \u2014 Tarnovar\u2019s frontier with the Brekelands \u2014 and made it the safest road in the fractured west by a method the whole continent knows: the Fence, a mile of stakes along the border hills, and on the stakes, oath-breakers. Bandits who violated surrender terms. Mercenaries who turned on their employers. A Brekeland captain who burned a village under parley-sign. Tarnovari law makes oath-breaking the one unforgivable crime; Dregan enforces the law. Merchants bless his name. Peasants cross the border toward him. The Voivode has never sanctioned the Fence and never dismantled it, and that silence is the central fact of Tarnovari politics. Speech pattern: soft, courteous, academy-perfect Imperial; never raises his voice; asks questions he already knows the answers to." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Dregan is the campaign\u2019s question about order made flesh \u2014 Qilvayas restores through law, Norr through conquest, Dregan through terror in the name of the kept word, and the roads are safest in Dregan\u2019s march. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "as a son of Threnvos\u2019s people, Dregan\u2019s obsessive punishment of oath-breakers is ancestral scar tissue he cannot name \u2014 and it has consequences he cannot see: the shadow, which feeds on broken oaths, finds the Eastmarch barren ground, and the nightmares that afflict oath-breakers cluster strangely at his border. He has noticed that the men he stakes die dreaming of a burning city. He has begun to wonder, in his cold patient way, what is listening. Of every soul on the board, Dregan is closest to guessing the campaign\u2019s secret from first principles." }]));

children.push(H3("Duchess Emerenn Vasq, n\u00e9e Meldane \u2014 the Dowager of the Suthmark"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Catherine de\u2019 Medici. ", i: true }, { t: "Born to Velmareth\u2019s great Meldane banking house, married young into the Suthmark\u2019s ducal line to seal a grain-and-credit compact, widowed at forty some fifteen years ago, and Dowager Duchess ever since \u2014 a style that carries no authority whatever. The Suthmark\u2019s duke is her elder son; every instrument of rule is his, and she has governed through her sons regardless \u2014 the sickly young Duke Aldous, and his ambitious younger brother, Tavian. The devout south never fully accepted the merchant\u2019s daughter, and she never asked it to; she governs through the Garland, her web of ladies-in-waiting, governesses, wine-factors, and priests\u2019 housekeepers, which knows what the Suthmark is thinking before the Suthmark does. Her defining wound is the Vintage Night, six years past: Orlathine congregations massacred in three towns during the harvest festival. She gave an order that night \u2014 what she meant by it and what it became are not the same thing \u2014 and rather than be seen to have lost control, she let the realm believe she meant all of it. The Suthmark fears her now. She has decided fear will serve. Speech pattern: exquisitely courteous; deploys her dead husband\u2019s opinions as cover for her own; switches to Velmarene idiom when she wants you to know the courtesy has ended." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Vasq holds the capital\u2019s food supply, which makes her patience the empire\u2019s most strategic resource. She bridges three regions \u2014 Meldane money in Velmareth, Suthmark grain, and the Orlath schism bleeding into her provinces \u2014 and she has quietly opened her own channels to Orlath, not from sympathy but because she hedges everything. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the truth of the Vintage Night order \u2014 now canon: a limited arrest that cascaded into massacre, which she chose to own rather than be seen to have lost control \u2014 is her breakable point \u2014 proof of what she actually said exists in a Garland lady\u2019s cipher-diary, and its surfacing would either humanize or destroy her. She knows about Countess Ory\u2019s hill country and its vanishing girls; she has done nothing, because the Ory estates hold Vasq debts, and she is waiting for the accusation to become profitable. The gothic horror in her south is, to her, an account not yet due." }]));

children.push(H3("The Suthmark\u2019s Sons \u2014 Duke Aldous and Tavian Vasq"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Duke Aldous Vasq is twenty-six, and has been dying since he was nine without ever managing it \u2014 a wasting complaint of the chest that his mother has spent a fortune failing to cure, and that three certified physicians have confidently named three different things. He rules by signature from a warm room, and rules better than the capital assumes: he reads every instrument that crosses his desk, forgets nothing that was in it, and his reeves are honest because they have learned he will notice. He has never ridden at the head of anything and never will. The Suthmark loves him the way a province loves a boy it expects to bury \u2014 completely, and without respect. Speech pattern: quiet, exact, faintly amused; asks the second question rather than the first." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Tavian is younger by four years, healthy, well-liked by precisely the wrong people, and entirely aware that the Denmother\u2019s Choice is revisable \u2014 that an heir is named by open declaration, and can be named again. He is not plotting against his brother. He is waiting, which is worse, because waiting asks nothing of him he would ever have to confess. Speech pattern: warm, generous, agrees early and often; the only man in the south who never mentions his brother\u2019s health." }]));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the succession is the Suthmark\u2019s fault line, and it runs directly beneath the Vintage Night. Emerenn governs on her sons\u2019 sufferance and everyone politely pretends otherwise; if the Garland lady\u2019s cipher-diary ever surfaces, the question of who actually rules the south becomes urgent in the same hour it becomes public. Neither son knows what their mother ordered that night. One of them has stopped asking." }]));

children.push(H3("Countess Velsanna Ory of Greywell"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Elizabeth B\u00e1thory. ", i: true }, { t: "The Ory family has held the grey hill country of the eastern Suthmark for four hundred years, and Castle Greywell has always been the kind of place the valley folk are relieved not to be invited to. Countess Velsanna is the last of the main line: a widow of vast estates and vaster loans \u2014 the crown itself owes the Ory treasury sums it has no intention of repaying \u2014 famously charitable at a distance, famously beautiful in a way that does not change. Her age is a local superstition: church records make her seventy; travelers who glimpse her at her gates describe a woman of forty. Servant girls take the good wages at Greywell and, some seasons, do not come home. The families are paid grief-silver. The local priest writes letters that are never answered. Nobody with power has ever had a reason to look, and everyone without power has excellent reasons not to. Speech pattern: gracious, old-fashioned, faintly amused, with the unhurried attention of something that has never once needed to rush." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Greywell is the campaign\u2019s pure gothic horror \u2014 deliberately unconnected to the shadow, because a living world grows its own evils, and the party should not be able to assume every darkness is the metaplot. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the horror is real. Velsanna sustains her youth by old blood-rite sorcery [mechanism to be detailed if she enters play \u2014 a hag-bargain, a fiendish compact, and an inherited rite are all viable; choose when needed], and the count of the missing runs past forty girls across two decades. The bitter irony preserved from history: when power finally moves against her, it will move for the debts, not the dead \u2014 and the party may be the only force in the world that ever comes for the girls\u2019 sake. As a villain she is beatable at almost any tier; as an institution \u2014 the silence, the grief-silver, the unanswered letters \u2014 she is a preview of every fight the campaign will ever pose." }]));

children.push(H3("Saint-Regent Olvesa the Reconciled \u2014 Founder of the See of Orlath"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Olga of Kiev. ", i: true }, { t: "Sixty years ago she was the young widow-regent of a northern principality whose lord had been murdered under guest-right by the Drevic clan-lords \u2014 and what she did about it is still told in the north in four movements, each worse than the last: the envoys buried alive with their gifts; the lodge burned with the second delegation feasting inside it; the funeral-feast on her husband\u2019s barrow where three hundred Drevic notables drank themselves helpless among her soldiers; and the birds \u2014 the tribute of sparrows and doves from every Drevic roof, released home at dusk with fire tied to their feet. Then, with her vengeance complete and her name a horror, she walked into a mountain shrine of the Matron and did not come out for a year. What came out was the woman the north now calls the Reconciled \u2014 and the See of Orlath founded itself around her rather than by her, penitents and clergy-refugees gathering to the shrine faster than she could send them home. She is its founder the way bedrock founds a house: she never set out to build a church, and the church was simply what would not leave. She set down her sword and her armor in the same season she gave up the Drevic tongue, and has taken up none of the three since \u2014 architect of the See\u2019s plain and rigorous rite almost against her will, and the nearest thing the fractured world has to a living saint, a title she accepts with visible distaste because she alone keeps the full ledger of what it cost. Her other title is stranger and more precise. The See holds the Lupine Throne vacant and its emperor illegitimate, and has done since its founding \u2014 which leaves a seat that must be kept by someone until it can be lawfully filled. Olvesa is Saint-Regent because she keeps it: regent of the True Rite, placeholder for an emperor not yet crowned, for sixty years. She rejects the capital Church not from doctrine first but from knowledge: she has seen, from the inside, exactly what absolution in the service of power looks like. Speech pattern: plain, spare, scripture-cadenced; never euphemizes her own past \u2014 refers to the four vengeances as \u201Cmy instruction,\u201D meaning what taught her." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Olvesa is the campaign\u2019s test of whether redemption is real \u2014 a genuinely holy woman with a river of blood behind her, leading a genuinely purer church toward a genuinely catastrophic act. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "her visions are true. Fragments of the storm and the wrong fire have reached her for decades, and three years ago \u2014 the season Nyreeza vanished \u2014 they darkened sharply. She reads them as the Matron\u2019s verdict on the corrupt capital and its oath-broken throne, and it is this conviction, not ambition, that has moved the coronation of her grandson from someday to now. She is, in perfect sincerity, about to crown a rival emperor because the shadow\u2019s stirring told her to. If anyone could ever show her what the visions actually are, the entire northern crisis would turn on that conversation. Note also what the coronation costs her personally: the moment Karvel is crowned, the regency she has held for sixty years dissolves, its purpose discharged. She has been keeping a chair warm for the grandson she is about to seat in it, and she knows it. Play her as a woman completing her life\u2019s work, not as one grasping at a new one." }]));

children.push(H3("King Karvel of Ardven \u2014 the Uncrowned Emperor of the North"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: Charlemagne. ", i: true }, { t: "Grandson of Saint-Regent Olvesa, king of a realm he assembled himself: fifteen years of war, marriage, and administration that turned a dozen northern crowns and clan-lands into Ardven. Karvel is broad, loud, semi-lettered and obsessed with letters \u2014 he reads slowly and has founded forty monastery schools; he sleeps four hours and audits his reeves personally; he prays like a soldier and legislates like a clerk. He is, in every way the capital would find uncomfortable to admit, what Qilvayas is trying to become: a unifier whose restoration is actually working. And this coming Solacre, if nothing intervenes, his grandmother\u2019s church will crown him Emperor of the True Rite \u2014 a second empire, sanctified by a second church, in direct and explicit denial of the Lupine Throne\u2019s mandate. Karvel himself is ambivalent in private: he wants the crown\u2019s legitimacy for his heirs, distrusts the theology, and suspects \u2014 correctly \u2014 that the coronation makes eventual war with the south very hard to avoid. Speech pattern: booming, blunt, unexpectedly precise on numbers; quotes scripture slightly wrong and dares anyone to correct him." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: the coronation is the campaign\u2019s second structural clock, set for the same year as the Zhuvedian Laws\u2019 promulgation \u2014 two rival covenants, sworn at opposite ends of the world, in the same season [" }, DM("DM note: "), { t: "the collision of these clocks is deliberate; whatever else the mid-campaign holds, that year should feel like a hinge of history]. Karvel is not a villain: he is a good king building a rival answer to the same question Qilvayas is asking, and the party may find his answer persuasive. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "a coronation is an oath sworn before a god \u2014 and the entity beneath Aenodira, born from a broken covenant at an empire\u2019s founding, will be listening with two thousand years of attention when a new empire swears its founding covenant. What that means in practice is deliberately open: design the consequence when the campaign approaches it." }]));

children.push(H3("Duke Garvin Norr \u2014 the Bastard of Normere"));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Historical key: William the Conqueror. ", i: true }, { t: "Baseborn son of the old Duke of Normere, acknowledged but never legitimized, who survived three assassination attempts before the age of twenty, killed the last of his trueborn rivals in the field at twenty-four, and has spent the two decades since building the most ruthlessly administered state west of Aenodira. Norr\u2019s conquest of the Brekelands proceeds warlord by warlord, and it is not pillage \u2014 it is absorption: every conquered holding is surveyed into the Reckoning Book, every landholder swears the personal oath to Norr himself over the heads of all intermediate lords, and every sworn man\u2019s obligations, taxes, and levies are recorded, audited, and enforced. Where submission is offered, terms are kept to the letter. Where it is refused \u2014 the Harrowing of the Weld, four years ago, ended a district\u2019s rebellion by ending, for a generation, the district. He claims descent from an old imperial governor\u2019s line and styles his conquest \u201Cthe restoration of lawful administration,\u201D which has the special insolence of being partly true. Speech pattern: flat, economical, deliberately unceremonious; recites exact figures from memory as intimidation; the word \u201Cbastard\u201D goes unremarked in his presence exactly once." }]));
children.push(PS([{ t: "Threads: Norr is Qilvayas\u2019s dark mirror \u2014 restoration by conquest and ledger against restoration by law and consent \u2014 and the empire\u2019s most dangerous rival precisely because his subjects\u2019 lives measurably improve. His westward absorption of the Brekelands is on a collision course with Ban Dregan\u2019s Eastmarch, and what happens when the Reckoning Book meets the Fence is one of the campaign\u2019s promised storms. " }, DM("DM Only: "), { t: "the personal oaths are the detail that matters. Norr has bound tens of thousands of souls to himself by sworn word \u2014 the largest concentration of living oaths in the fractured world \u2014 and he keeps his side of every one of them, scrupulously, as policy. He has, without knowing it, built either the shadow\u2019s greatest feast (if he ever breaks faith at scale) or the strongest oath-bound bulwark on the continent (if he never does). A single great betrayal by Norr would feed the entity beyond anything since the fracture began. He must never learn that. Certain other parties, late in the campaign, might try to arrange it." }]));

// Geography
children.push(H1("Geography and Locations"));
children.push(H2("Aenodira: The Imperial Capital"));
children.push(P("Aenodira sits where the Ostrun bends into a wide horseshoe against its northern approach, and the city has grown, ring by ring, to fill everything the river\u2019s curve allows and then some. Three walls circle it, each raised in a different age of the empire and each still wearing the color of the age that built it: the Founder\u2019s Wall, innermost and oldest, dark and roughly hewn; the High Wall, pale gold limestone raised at the height of imperial wealth, wide-gated and half ceremonial; and the Long Wall, outermost, red brick thrown up fast during the empire\u2019s last great expansion \u2014 the one that came right before the fracture began. Walking outward from the palace is walking forward through two thousand years of the empire\u2019s confidence, ring by ring, until you reach the wall it built for a city that never came."));
children.push(P("Aenodira is inland, definitively: well up the Ostrun\u2019s valley, with river trade through Rivergate but no ocean harbor of any kind \u2014 the nearest salt water is Velmareth\u2019s delta, ten days downriver. Every approach is therefore a road or the river, and which one shapes a traveler before they ever see the walls: four days from the Ostmark through loyalist country that still mostly functions; two weeks from the Brekelands, where banditry is the ambient condition rather than the exception; three weeks down from Tarnovar\u2019s highlands, where the Fence\u2019s deterrence ends at Fencegate and does not follow you into the Crownlands; or up the Ostrun itself from Velmareth \u2014 river traffic with a different flavor of risk entirely, tolls and smugglers and whatever operates out of Rivergate\u2019s quieter berths, rather than anything so honest as highway robbery."));
children.push(P("The Long Wall was built on the assumption that the city would keep growing to fill it. It didn\u2019t. The result is an outer city that is unevenly occupied \u2014 half-finished districts standing empty beside others crammed well past any sensible density \u2014 and that unevenness, more than any single landmark, is what the outer ring is actually about."));

children.push(H2("The Inner City"));
children.push(P("Contained within the Founder\u2019s Wall, the oldest heart of Aenodira is where the empire\u2019s two great authorities face each other across a shallow valley: the palace on one rise, the Temple on the other, close enough that a bell rung in one is heard clearly in the other \u2014 which everyone who has ever needed to negotiate between throne and altar has found either convenient or ominous, depending on which side of the negotiation they were on."));
children.push(H3("Highcourt"));
children.push(P("The palace district, called Highcourt by everyone except the clerks who insist on \u201Cthe Palatine Precinct\u201D in official correspondence, holds the Lupine Throne, the ministries that keep what remains of the empire\u2019s paperwork running, and the townhouses of whatever nobility still bothers to maintain a capital residence. It is guarded, aloof, and quieter than a city this size has any right to be at its literal center \u2014 quiet in the particular way of a place where everyone is watching everyone else\u2019s movements and nobody wants to be the one who made noise."));
children.push(P("The Long Course, a broad ceremonial avenue and parade ground running along Highcourt\u2019s eastern edge, is where the legions march on imperial holidays, where new laws are read aloud to whatever public gathers to hear them, and where the proclamations of the coming Zhuvedian Laws are currently posted at every crossing. It is also, not incidentally, the traditional site of public executions, though the current reign has used it for that purpose only twice."));
children.push(B("Power and Presence:", "Access to the Emperor himself runs through Lord Chamberlain Vareth Kessin, a career bureaucrat who has outlasted two regents and one attempted reform of his own office, and who treats every petitioner\u2019s business as simultaneously vital and faintly beneath him. Nothing reaches Qilvayas without passing Kessin\u2019s desk first, which makes Kessin one of the most quietly powerful people in the empire and one of the least visible."));
children.push(H3("The Sanctum"));
children.push(P("Across the valley from Highcourt, the Sanctum climbs the second of the city\u2019s two hills toward the golden dome of the Great Temple of the Matron \u2014 visible, on a clear day, from every ring of the city and from the pilgrim road for a day\u2019s travel before arrival. Beneath and around the Temple proper spreads the Church\u2019s actual machinery: administrative halls, seminaries, the residences of a clergy that ranges from genuinely devout to comfortably political, and the Office of Omens, whose officials would very much like to be the empire\u2019s sole authority on visions, portents, and divine will, and who resent, with real institutional bitterness, that they are not."));
children.push(B("Power and Presence:", "Matriarch Ilsevet Corvane, Voice of the Matron in the capital, is the Church\u2019s most senior public figure in Aenodira \u2014 not its supreme authority (that distinction is more contested, and more interesting, than any single title suggests), but its face, its administrator, and the person any official inquiry into the party\u2019s shared vision would eventually land on. She is neither villain nor ally by default; she is institution, and institutions protect themselves first and ask questions about truth second. The Office of Omens answers to her, nominally."));
children.push(H3("The Old Forum"));
children.push(P("Tucked against the inside of the Founder\u2019s Wall, in the oldest and least renovated corner of the Inner City, the Old Forum is what remains of Aenodira before it was a capital \u2014 a warren of foundation-stones, half-buried columns, and one weathered monument to Zhuvedus himself, worn smooth by two thousand years of hands touching it for luck on the way to somewhere else. Scholars catalogue it. Beggars and the genuinely destitute shelter in it. Nobody has excavated it properly in living memory, because proper excavation would mean admitting how little the Church and the imperial cult actually agree on what they\u2019d find there."));
children.push(PS([DM("DM Only: "), { t: "Confirmed canon: the binding site lies beneath the Old Forum\u2019s foundations, reached through the catacombs that everyone knows exist and nobody has fully mapped, descending into the Undercourt\u2019s deepest reach. All future site design builds on this as fixed ground." }]));
children.push(H3("The Undercourt (DM Only)"));
children.push(P("Aenodira stands on Aenodira. Beneath the modern city lies a connected pre-imperial stratum \u2014 the Undercourt \u2014 the buried remains of whatever stood here before Zhuvedus\u2019s founding: black roughly-hewn stone of the same make as the Founder\u2019s Wall, streets three man-heights below the current ones, and chambers that predate every map the empire keeps. The catacombs under the Old Forum touch it. So, in places, do the deep vaults under Scholar\u2019s Row \u2014 including the academy\u2019s decommissioned Undervault, which matters in Sessions Three and Four. The Church has never excavated it; the imperial cult has never asked to; both institutions\u2019 disinterest is older than anyone can account for and functions, in practice, like a door everyone has agreed not to try."));
children.push(P("In the Undercourt\u2019s deepest reach lies the binding site \u2014 the place the oath was broken and Threnvos died. The descent as far as the Second Seal is now built and canon (see Session Six); what lies beyond the Seal remains the next major build. Established features: cold that deepens with proximity (the same cold as Dren\u2019s medallion); pre-imperial inscriptions in a script the Church does not teach; and evidence, in at least two places, of a careful modern visitor \u2014 hairline survey marks in Empress Nyreeza\u2019s personal cipher, three years old. She found a way down. Whether she found a way back up is the campaign\u2019s open wound."));

children.push(H2("The Middle City"));
children.push(P("Beyond the High Wall, Aenodira does its actual work. This is where most of the city\u2019s population lives, trades, studies, and prays without any particular proximity to power \u2014 and where the party will spend most of their time in the capital."));
children.push(H3("Coppergate"));
children.push(P("Already familiar to the party by the time they reach it: copyists, instrument-makers, retired functionaries, laundry strung between balconies, and, above a copyist\u2019s shop on Ninth Lane, the blue-shuttered apartment of Vaelindra of the Still Waters. Coppergate is comfortable, unglamorous, and exactly the kind of place someone chooses when they want to be findable only by people who already know where to look."));
children.push(H3("Scholar\u2019s Row"));
children.push(P("Home to the Imperial Academy of the Lupine Throne \u2014 swept courts, ringing bells, restricted archives kept by Archivist Dathenor Vell \u2014 along with the smaller tutoring houses, instrument shops, and cheap lodging-houses that cluster around any institution full of young people with stipends. The party\u2019s dormitory years live here. So, less pleasantly, does the reputation the academy has spent decades trying to shed: everyone in Scholar\u2019s Row has an opinion about which students are lottery winners and which are hostages, and most of those opinions are wrong in interesting ways."));
children.push(H3("The Exchange"));
children.push(P("Aenodira\u2019s guildhalls, counting-houses, and covered markets, where what remains of the empire\u2019s trade actually changes hands. Merchant Republic delegations keep permanent offices here \u2014 officially trade missions, unofficially outposts of exactly the disorder-preferring interests already described under the empire\u2019s current political landscape."));
children.push(B("Power and Presence:", "Guildmaster Ptolan Vess, head of the Capital Merchants\u2019 Concord, is the closest thing the Exchange has to a single voice, though \u201Cclosest thing\u201D undersells how little the guilds actually agree with each other. Vess is pragmatic, genuinely apolitical by temperament, and privately convinced that Qilvayas\u2019s restoration project is bad for business regardless of whether it succeeds."));
children.push(H3("The Garrison"));
children.push(P("Barracks, drill yards, and the administrative offices of whatever legion elements are currently garrisoned in the capital \u2014 a smaller and more ceremonial presence than the empire once kept here, which everyone in the Garrison is uncomfortably aware of."));
children.push(B("Power and Presence:", "Legate Bruvasca Thorne commands the Palatine Guard, the capital\u2019s actual defensive force, and maintains a professional, faintly weary correspondence with provincial magistrates \u2014 including, as it happens, Cassivar Ondrei of Dravenna, whose complaint against the Third Legion\u2019s colonel is currently sitting somewhere in Thorne\u2019s inbox, exactly as slow-moving as every other piece of institutional accountability in the empire."));

children.push(H2("The Outer City"));
children.push(H3("The Archwork"));
children.push(P("An old imperial aqueduct crosses the outer city on a run of high stone arches, most of it still functional, several sections long since bricked up and converted into multi-story housing wedged directly into the arch spans. Rent is cheap because the housing is, structurally speaking, a repurposed water pipe, and everyone who lives there knows it. Nickname aside, the Archwork is a working-class district in the ordinary sense \u2014 laborers, small tradespeople, the families of Garrison rank-and-file who can\u2019t afford anything closer to the center."));
children.push(H3("Farrowgate"));
children.push(P("The newest and least planned district in the city, growing without much municipal help along the Long Wall\u2019s inner face, populated overwhelmingly by displaced people from the fractured territories \u2014 not any single group, but a slow accretion of every border skirmish, warlord dispute, and burned village that has sent someone walking toward the capital because an old story said the throne feeds its own. Established Aenodirans call it Farrowgate without much affection; the people who live there increasingly use the name themselves, the way a slur gets worn down into an identity by people who didn\u2019t get to choose it."));
children.push(P("This is not where Semya\u2019s people from Halvenne ended up, if the party helped resettle them in Dravenna \u2014 but it is exactly where they would have ended up if the party hadn\u2019t."));
children.push(H3("Rivergate"));
children.push(P("The docks and river trade quarter, where goods \u2014 and a fair amount that isn\u2019t quite goods \u2014 move in and out of the capital. Less prestigious than the Exchange, less regulated, and considerably more interesting to anyone investigating something the empire\u2019s official record doesn\u2019t want to explain, including, plausibly, wherever Odric Hale bought his dead Roads Commission seal."));

children.push(PS([{ t: "None of this is exhaustive, and it isn\u2019t meant to be \u2014 Aenodira should grow the way real capitals do, one specific street or shopfront named the moment a scene actually needs it, built outward from this framework rather than pre-mapped down to the alley. What\u2019s fixed for now: the three rings and their walls, the two hills of Highcourt and the Sanctum, Coppergate\u2019s location relative to the Academy, and the Old Forum as the confirmed location above the binding site " }, DM("(DM only)."), { t: "" }]));
children.push(P("Whatever lies beneath the capital \u2014 the likely site of the broken oath and Threnvos\u2019s death (see The Founding Myth: Zhuvedus and the Broken Oath) \u2014 should be developed alongside the capital itself, since the two are now the same design problem."));

// Plot Hooks
children.push(H1("Plot Hooks and Adventure Seeds"));
children.push(P("Seeds already planted by Sessions Zero through Four, ready to grow when the table needs them. Each is deliberately small enough to run as a side thread and connected enough to pay into the main arc."));
children.push(BUL("The Colonel\u2019s Reckoning:", "Ondrei\u2019s complaint against Colonel Aurel Dessen of the Third Legion grinds through Legate Thorne\u2019s office. The colonel has friends, the surviving deserters\u2019 testimony needs protecting, and the party is the reason there is a case at all. A courtroom-and-intimidation arc in the Ostmark \u2014 and a test of whether the system Qilvayas is rebuilding can actually convict one of its own."));
children.push(BUL("The Seal Dealer of Rivergate (engaged: Session Five):", "Odric Hale\u2019s dead Roads Commission seal came from somewhere. The dealer trades in the seals, signets, and letterheads of abolished imperial offices \u2014 and someone has recently bought the seals of offices that handled the old censuses of the Founder\u2019s Wall district. Who wants to forge authority over the oldest records in the city, and why now?"));
children.push(BUL("Forty Witnesses:", "Semya\u2019s people, resettled in Dravenna (or struggling in Farrowgate if not), become a listening post and a responsibility. When trouble reaches them \u2014 a landlord, a press-gang, a recruiter for someone\u2019s private army \u2014 the party learns what their earlier kindness actually bought, and what it obligates."));
children.push(BUL("The Office of Omens Moves (engaged: Sessions Five\u2013Six):", "Prelate Odell\u2019s office hears of a full academy cohort, a shared dream, and an unsanctioned consultation in Coppergate \u2014 and under its new posture, it does not merely open a file. Surveillance, a summons with teeth, and ultimately a Writ of Examination against Vaelindra herself. The courtesy remains; the patience does not. How the party handles being hunted by paperwork shapes their standing with the Church for the entire campaign."));
children.push(BUL("The Cold Door (engaged: Session Six):", "The unscripted door in the Undervault (Sessions Three and Four) does not go away. Masons re-seal the wing; the party holds a rubbing of cipher marks Vell can identify as the Empress\u2019s hand. The first thread of the Nyreeza trail, and the campaign\u2019s standing invitation to go beneath the city."));
children.push(BUL("A Clerk from Highcourt (engaged: Session Five):", "Someone from Chamberlain Kessin\u2019s office observed the Proving. The newly chartered company receives its first real commission \u2014 modest, deniable, and a test with the throne\u2019s fingerprints kept carefully off it. Passing it opens the palace door an inch."));
children.push(BUL("The Tarnovar Envoy:", "A rare Tarnovari delegation comes to Aenodira \u2014 trade terms, nominally \u2014 and its members unsettle everyone: they will not swear on the Matron, they touch stone before signing anything, and one of their singers performs a ballad of Vosthren the Betrayed within earshot of the wrong scholar. A slow fuse to the campaign\u2019s largest buried revelation."));
children.push(BUL("The Medallion Grows Colder:", "Dren\u2019s defaced oath-medallion is a barometer nobody knows how to read yet. Days when it is colder correlate \u2014 the party will eventually notice \u2014 with news of some notable broken faith somewhere in the fracturing empire. A quiet, persistent instrument for seeding the central mechanism without a word of exposition."));
children.push(BUL("The Landless King\u2019s Fifth Petition:", "Sea-King Aldrec sends envoys to Aenodira one more time \u2014 tally-sticks, formal Imperial, and an offer to hold the coast against his own displaced kin in exchange for a march and a title. The court means to refuse him again. Anyone \u2014 the party included \u2014 who grasps what is actually being offered has a narrow window to change the answer before the window closes with Aldrec\u2019s failing health."));
children.push(BULLET([
  { t: "Solacre Day: ", b: true },
  { t: "The promulgation isn\u2019t three developments that happen to share a season \u2014 it\u2019s one convergent day. Qilvayas reads the Zhuvedian Laws aloud from the Long Course as scheduled; Book One\u2019s language on the Marked lands in that same reading, whichever way the party tipped it; Book Three\u2019s registration requirement, newly extended over Long Course commerce, takes effect in front of the crowd it affects most (see The Grey-Gold Rising, below); and within the same day, word reaches the capital that Karvel has been crowned in Orlath (see The Coronation Clock, below). Three structural clocks, one bell." }
]));
children.push(BULLET([
  { t: "The Grey-Gold Rising: ", b: true },
  { t: "The spark isn\u2019t a new tax \u2014 it\u2019s Book Three\u2019s existing mark-and-register requirement, extended for the first time over Long Course commerce: stables, trainers, tipsters, and bookmakers now need a Crown-guild register entry like any other mundane trade, with the same burden-shifting teeth as everywhere else. The Golds \u2014 the inner-wall houses, patron money and breeding stock \u2014 resent an inspector auditing what used to run on handshake and patronage. The Greys \u2014 the outer-wall trade, concession workers and small bookmakers who\u2019ve never registered anything with anyone \u2014 are the ones actually hauled before a magistrate for lacking an entry they never knew to make. It takes one well-liked name made an example of at the Course itself before two factions who\u2019ve hated each other for generations discover they hate this more. What follows is a capital riot in the Justinianic mold: the day Qilvayas must choose between mercy and massacre, with the party positioned wherever their choices have put them. " },
  DM("DM Only: "),
  { t: "deliberately live, tracked on the Branch Ledger exactly like the Marked\u2019s personhood \u2014 what the party has actually built by the time it breaks (Thorne\u2019s room to maneuver, how much warning Qilvayas gets, who\u2019s physically present and what they do) tips it either way. A massacre feeds the thing beneath the city at real scale; a mercied Rising is the Laws doing, for the first time in public, the one thing they were built to do. Don\u2019t manufacture the spark before the party\u2019s choices have given it a direction to fall." }
]));
children.push(BULLET([
  { t: "The Coronation Clock: ", b: true },
  { t: "Orlath crowns King Karvel Emperor of the True Rite this coming Solacre \u2014 the same day, not merely the same season, as the Laws\u2019 promulgation (see Solacre Day, above). Two rival covenants, sworn at opposite ends of the world, arriving in the capital\u2019s ears within the same hour. Embassies, sabotage, desperate diplomacy, and the question nobody in the capital is asking: why now? (The DM knows: Olvesa\u2019s visions darkened three years ago. See The Powers of the Fractured Empire.)" }
]));
children.push(BUL("The Girls of Greywell:", "A Farrowgate family begs the newly chartered company to find a daughter who took service in the Suthmark hills and stopped writing. The trail leads to Castle Greywell, grief-silver, unanswered letters \u2014 and a horror with four hundred years of rank and no connection at all to the shadow. Some evils are just evils. The party may be the first force that ever comes for the girls\u2019 sake."));
children.push(BUL("The Book and the Fence:", "Norr\u2019s surveyors reach the eastern Brekeland hills within sight of Ban Dregan\u2019s stakes. Two absolute systems of order regard each other across a valley. Whoever is standing between them \u2014 refugees, merchants, a party on a job \u2014 gets to watch history decide whether the Reckoning Book and the Fence can share a border."));

children.push(H1("The Imperial Calendar"));
children.push(P("Twelve months, opening at the year\u2019s turn in deep winter with the Matron\u2019s own liturgical season, running the plain agricultural cycle everyone actually lives by, and closing with her second high month before the year turns again."));
children.push(table(
  ["Month", "Season", "Character"],
  [24, 22, 54],
  [
    ["Wolfmoon", "Deep winter (year begins)", "The Matron\u2019s high liturgical month; the year turns in her keeping, not the sun\u2019s."],
    ["Thawtide", "Late winter", "Roads reopen; the Ostmark\u2019s watch-forts change garrison."],
    ["Sowmonth", "Early spring", "Suthmark planting begins; the year\u2019s first oaths of service are traditionally sworn."],
    ["Greening", "Mid-spring", "Long Course racing season opens (Golds vs. Greys)."],
    ["Solacre", "Early summer", "Court business in full swing; the Zhuvedian Laws are slated for a Solacre reading."],
    ["Haymonth", "Midsummer", "Rural labor peak; Academy field exercises traditionally run Hay\u2013Harvestide."],
    ["Harvestide", "Early autumn", "The Suthmark\u2019s great harvest festival \u2014 and the Vintage Night fell in Harvestide."],
    ["Vinmoon", "Mid-autumn", "Grape harvest; Suthmark vintages are dated by Vinmoon year."],
    ["Fallowmonth", "Late autumn", "Fields rest; Suthmark inheritance customarily settles at Fallowmonth\u2019s turn."],
    ["Greywane", "Early winter", "Days shorten; the Long Course racing season closes."],
    ["Longdark", "Deep winter", "The Matron\u2019s second high month, mirroring Wolfmoon; Vigil Halls keep continuous watch."],
    ["Threshold", "Year\u2019s end", "A short, liminal month of 20 days \u2014 old debts settled, old oaths renewed, before Wolfmoon turns the year."]
  ]
));
children.push(P("Loyalist territory counts Years of the Reckoning (YR) from the Founding, the exact figure left deliberately soft. Orlath keeps YR for trade and secular record-keeping but privately dates its liturgical documents from the Reconciliation, Olvesa\u2019s year in the shrine \u2014 even the schismatics haven\u2019t stopped using the empire\u2019s clock, they\u2019ve simply started keeping a second one. Tarnovar mostly dispenses with numbered years for internal purposes, marking time by successive Voivodes\u2019 reigns instead. A light seven-day week \u2014 a market day, a rest day tied to Vigil-keeping, five working days \u2014 completes the structure without inventing texture nobody will use at the table."));

// Timeline
children.push(H1("Campaign Timeline and Pacing"));
children.push(P("Chronology of established events, relative to the campaign\u2019s present day (the party\u2019s final field exercise, six weeks before commencement). Dates are framed as \u201Cyears ago\u201D to stay portable if a calendar is adopted later."));
children.push(BUL("~2,000 years ago:", "The founding. Zhuvedus breaks the sworn covenant, destroys Threnvos, and establishes the empire alone. The binding site seals whatever remained. Threnvos\u2019s surviving people scatter west; their descendants become Tarnovar."));
children.push(BUL("~200 years ago:", "The fracture begins. Provinces fall away over the following two centuries; every abandoned fealty and torn treaty feeds what waits beneath the capital."));
children.push(BUL("Across the two centuries since:", "The throne turns inward as the provinces turn away. The Lupine Throne changes hands far faster than a Drow line should \u2014 reigns cut short by fever, by faction, by an unlucky border or a fall on the Long Course, twice falling to child-heirs and the regents who ruled in their place. The court names a cause for each and never counts them together. Nyreeza, ruling in her own right until a son grown enough to follow her without a regent, is the first steadiness the throne has known in longer than most can remember. (See the Packlaw; and DM Only, The Scouring.)"));
children.push(BUL("~60 years ago:", "The Silvasse Disaster: three legions and their wolf-standards lost in the western woods \u2014 the fracture\u2019s military nadir, and the wound Marshal Dane\u2019s recovery of the Ninth\u2019s standard has begun, sixty years on, to heal."));
children.push(BUL("~60\u201358 years ago:", "In the north: the murder of Olvesa\u2019s lord under guest-right, her four vengeances against the Drevic clans, her year in the mountain shrine, and the See of Orlath founding itself around her on her return (~58 years ago)."));
children.push(BUL("~50 years ago:", "Vaelindra\u2019s career-ending vision \u2014 fragments of the true founding \u2014 and her quiet removal from the Church. She begins her independent ledger of visions."));
children.push(BUL("~40 years ago:", "A young Dregan Morn arrives at the capital academy as a hostage-student of Tarnovar, and stays on past his studies, as hostages do, at the throne\u2019s convenience; he returns home a decade later, changed."));
children.push(BUL("~20 years ago:", "The Weeping Strait: an imperial admiral spends three thousand Skell auxiliaries to save his squadrons. Aldrec survives, is denied his promised command, and begins his kingship of the displaced. In the west, Garvin Norr kills the last of his trueborn rivals and takes Normere in fact."));
children.push(BUL("~15 years ago:", "Karvel begins the consolidation of the northern crowns into Ardven; the ship-clans lose their fjords over the following years and turn south."));
children.push(BUL("~12 years ago:", "Empress Nyreeza begins the academy revival and the first institutional reforms \u2014 and, somewhere in these years, her private inquiry into the founding."));
children.push(BUL("11 years ago:", "Archivist Vell\u2019s last referral to Vaelindra \u2014 the one that ended badly. He has not presumed on her since."));
children.push(BUL("6 years ago:", "The Vintage Night: Orlathine congregations massacred in three Suthmark towns during the harvest festival. Duchess Vasq owns an order she may not have meant. The south learns to fear her."));
children.push(BUL("4 years ago:", "The Harrowing of the Weld: Duke Norr ends a district\u2019s rebellion, and the district, for a generation. The west learns what his patience costs."));
children.push(BUL("3 years ago:", "Nyreeza\u2019s final meeting with Vaelindra; her disappearance shortly after. Vaelindra\u2019s ledger records the vision-fragments accelerating from this season \u2014 and, unknown to the capital, Saint-Regent Olvesa\u2019s visions darken the same season, setting the coronation in motion. A six-month search and interregnum follows."));
children.push(BULLET([DM("~3 years ago (DM only): "), { t: "In the western wood, Vessarkath lets one wolf-standard leave her hoard \u2014 she felt the stir beneath the capital (see The Acceleration); through the Brekelands warlords, the Ninth\u2019s eagle begins its slow way home." }]));
children.push(BUL("~2.5 years ago:", "Qilvayas\u2019s coronation. The restoration project begins in earnest: the Law Commission under Archjurist Vhal, the academy expansion, the diplomatic overtures."));
children.push(BUL("~1 year ago:", "Marshal Dane recovers the Ninth\u2019s standard from the Brekelands, nearly sixty years after Silvasse swallowed it. The Ostmark\u2019s deepest wound begins to close, and the empire gets its Young Wolf."));
children.push(BUL("This coming Solacre, nine months out:", "Two clocks, one day: the Zhuvedian Laws promulgated from the Long Course \u2014 Book One\u2019s language on the Marked inside that same reading, and Book Three\u2019s registration falling on Long Course commerce in front of the crowd it costs most \u2014 and, within the same hours, word from Orlath that Karvel has been crowned Emperor of the True Rite. Rival covenants at opposite ends of the world, arriving in the capital\u2019s ears on one bell: the restoration\u2019s point of no return, and its first true rival. Every faction in the empire is already positioning."));
children.push(BUL("Present day:", "The party\u2019s field exercise, the vision, the return to Aenodira, and the Proving. Commencement \u2014 moved up for the Proven cohort \u2014 follows Session Four."));
children.push(P("Pacing guidance: the Zhuvedian Laws promulgation is the campaign\u2019s first structural clock \u2014 events the party does not attend to will still move toward it. The three-year freshness of Nyreeza\u2019s disappearance means her trail is warm: witnesses remember, her cipher marks are recent, and her son\u2019s grief is a live political fact rather than settled history. Vaelindra\u2019s correlation \u2014 the visions accelerating from the season the Empress vanished \u2014 is the connective tissue the party can discover early, and it is true: whatever Nyreeza did at the binding site changed something."));

